        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Ghidra analysis stack for BFM PS1 decomp: ghidra_psx_ldr (PSX loader/PSY-Q signatures) + GhidraMCP (Claude Code MCP bridge) — versions, install, capabilities, compatibility",
        "key_facts": [
          "ghidra_psx_ldr (github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr): latest release tag 2026.06.04 ('Add Ghidra 12.1') ships prebuilt zips named ghidra_<GHIDRAVER>_PUBLIC_20260604_ghidra_psx_ldr.zip for Ghidra 12.0, 12.0.1, 12.0.2, 12.0.3, 12.0.4, 12.1 (~9.15 MB each, signatures bundled). Ghidra 11.x support ENDED at release 2026.01.04 ('Due to Ghidra API change, only 12.0+ versions will be compiled moving forward') whose assets cover 11.4/11.4.1/11.4.2/11.4.3; release 2025.06.28 attached the last artifacts for Ghidra 10.4–11.3.2.",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr install (binary): download the zip matching your EXACT Ghidra version -> Ghidra File -> Install Extensions... -> '+' -> select zip -> restart. Build-from-source path uses Eclipse + GhidraDev (Link Ghidra, then GhidraDev -> Export -> Ghidra module extension); if cloning source, the signature data is a git submodule (data/psyq -> lab313ru/psx_psyq_signatures), so clone --recursive.",
          "On PSX-EXE import ghidra_psx_ldr sets language 'PSX:LE:32:default' (custom SLEIGH incl. GTE/cop2 registers), loader name 'PSX Executables Loader', and builds the full PS1 memory map: RAM at 0x80000000 around the loaded image, CODE/.data/.bss from the PSX-EXE header, scratchpad CACHE 0x1F800000 (0x400), MCTRL1 0x1F801000, IO_PORTS 0x1F801040, INT_CTRL 0x1F801070, 7 DMA channel blocks from 0x1F801080, 3 timers from 0x1F801100, CDROM_REGS 0x1F801800, GPU_REGS 0x1F801810, SPU_VOICES 0x1F801C00 (0x180), SPU_CTRL_REGS 0x1F801D80 — plus a synthetic GTEMAC segment at 0x20000000 containing GTE-macro pseudo-functions (defined in data/gte_macro.json) so GTE macros decompile as named calls with register args.",
          "PSY-Q SDK version detection (src/main/java/psyq/DetectPsyQ.java): scans program memory for the 'Ps' version stamp (byte pattern 0x50 0x73 + masked bytes; library number at byte 3, version short read at offset +0x06), formats e.g. 0x4700 -> '4.7'; fallback for old SDKs tries versions 260/300/330/340/350 by matching LIBGPU.LIB SYS.OBJ JSON signatures against memory. Detected version is stored in program metadata: Edit -> Options for Program -> Program Information -> 'PsyQ Version' field (manually editable; append '.0' if you get a \"'psyq/xx' cannot be found\" error).",
          "PSY-Q signature matching: auto-enabled 'PsyQ Signatures' analyzer for PSX-language programs reads JSON signatures from the extension's data dir psyq/<VERSION>/*.json (excluding patches.json) via SigApplier; analyzer options: 'Only first match', 'Minimal signature entropy' (default 3.0, filters short/false-positive matches), and 'PsyQ Version if not found' (manual override). The plugin also ships .gdt data-type archives per SDK version (2.60–4.70 incl. 3.610/3.611) auto-loaded by loadPsyqGdt(), giving PSY-Q struct/typedef types.",
          "Signature source repo: github.com/lab313ru/psx_psyq_signatures — 'Signatures for Playstation 1 PsyQ LIB/OBJ files in JSON form', version dirs 260, 300, 330, 340, 350, 3610, 3611, 370, 400, 410, 420, 430, 440, 450, 460, 470, plus generator/ (Python signature generator), syscalls/, til/. These are ALREADY BUNDLED inside the ghidra_psx_ldr release zips — no separate OBJ download needed for signature matching. Raw PSY-Q SDK .LIB/.OBJ files (needed later for matching builds, not for the plugin) are commonly obtained from psx.arthus.net/sdk/ (Psy-Q archives) or archive.org PsyQ SDK mirrors.",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr can also import raw PSY-Q OBJ/LIB files directly into Ghidra: place an empty marker file next to them named PSYQ_LIBNAME_XXX (e.g. PSYQ_LIBSND_470) for OBJs extracted from a specific library, or PSYQ_XXX for standalone OBJs/batch import, where XXX is a version listed in psx_psyq_signatures.",
          "CreateGteMacSegment script (Script Manager -> 'PSX GTE' folder): only needed when MIGRATING an existing/legacy Ghidra project to the PSX language (set language to PSX, run script, reanalyze) — fresh imports create the GTEMAC segment automatically. Projects created before 2022-01-22 additionally need GTE instructions undefined and re-disassembled to regenerate pcode. Overlay workflow demo video: https://youtu.be/fpijiL1x3Pc.",
          "GhidraMCP (github.com/LaurieWired/GhidraMCP): latest official release is 1.4 (2025-06-23, asset GhidraMCP-release-1-4.zip, 31,180 bytes) targeting Ghidra 11.3.2. NO official build for Ghidra 11.4+ or 12.x as of 2026-06-10: issue #83 (open) documents that 1.4 fails on 11.4.1 due to Module.manifest format change ('Invalid line encountered: ModuleName=GhidraMCP'); issue #131 (open) requests Ghidra 12.1 support. Repo has 9.2k stars, 47 open issues, 33 open PRs — effectively low-maintenance.",
          "GhidraMCP install: File -> Install Extensions -> '+' -> release zip -> restart Ghidra -> File -> Configure -> Developer -> enable 'GhidraMCPPlugin' -> (optional) change port in Edit -> Tool Options -> 'GhidraMCP HTTP Server' (default http://127.0.0.1:8080/). The embedded HTTP server operates on the program currently open in CodeBrowser. Python bridge: bridge_mcp_ghidra.py, deps = 'pip install mcp requests' (imports mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP and requests); args: --ghidra-server (default http://127.0.0.1:8080/), --transport stdio|sse (default stdio), --mcp-host (127.0.0.1), --mcp-port (8081 for sse).",
          "Claude Code connection on Windows (project-scoped .mcp.json in repo root): {\"mcpServers\":{\"ghidra\":{\"command\":\"python\",\"args\":[\"Z:\\\\Storage\\\\git\\\\BFM-decomp\\\\tools\\\\GhidraMCP\\\\bridge_mcp_ghidra.py\",\"--ghidra-server\",\"http://127.0.0.1:8080/\"]}}} — or one-liner: claude mcp add ghidra -- python Z:\\path\\to\\bridge_mcp_ghidra.py --ghidra-server http://127.0.0.1:8080/  (stdio transport; Ghidra must be running with the program open before tools work).",
          "GhidraMCP 1.4 exposes exactly 27 MCP tools (from bridge_mcp_ghidra.py @mcp.tool defs): list_methods, list_classes, decompile_function (by name), rename_function, rename_data, list_segments, list_imports, list_exports, list_namespaces, list_data_items, search_functions_by_name, rename_variable, get_function_by_address, get_current_address, get_current_function, list_functions, decompile_function_by_address, disassemble_function, set_decompiler_comment, set_disassembly_comment, rename_function_by_address, set_function_prototype, set_local_variable_type, get_xrefs_to, get_xrefs_from, get_function_xrefs, list_strings. Pagination: offset=0/limit=100 defaults (list_strings limit=2000); list_functions has NO pagination.",
          "Coexistence: yes — the two are independent Ghidra extensions (PSX loader/analyzer/SLEIGH vs. an HTTP-server plugin) with no known conflicts; both install via File -> Install Extensions. Latest Ghidra version BOTH support with STOCK release binaries: Ghidra 11.3.2 (GhidraMCP 1.4 + ghidra_psx_ldr 2025.06.28 asset ghidra_11.3.2_*). To run current Ghidra 12.x (ghidra_psx_ldr official up to 12.1), GhidraMCP must come from a fork or self-build: ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12 (Ghidra 12.0.1 + JDK 21 fixes), bethington/ghidra-mcp (claims Ghidra 12.0.3 compat, 200+ tools, headless mode), or rebuild LaurieWired source: copy Base.jar, Decompiler.jar, Docking.jar, Generic.jar, Project.jar, SoftwareModeling.jar, Utility.jar, Gui.jar from the Ghidra install into lib/ then 'mvn clean package assembly:single' (JDK 21; fixes 'Source option 5 is no longer supported' per issue #99).",
          "Java requirements: Ghidra 11.3.x and all 12.x require JDK 21. Ghidra extension zips are version-locked via extension.properties — a zip built for a different point release shows red in the extension list and fails to load (this is why ghidra_psx_ldr publishes one zip per Ghidra point version)."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Decide Ghidra version: EITHER Ghidra 11.3.2 (both plugins work with unmodified official releases — lowest friction) OR Ghidra 12.0.x/12.1 (newest ghidra_psx_ldr, but GhidraMCP must be a fork or rebuilt from source with Maven/JDK 21).",
          "Install JDK 21 (required by Ghidra 11.3.2 and 12.x) and the chosen Ghidra release zip from github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/releases; run ghidraRun.bat natively on Windows.",
          "Install ghidra_psx_ldr: download the asset zip exactly matching the Ghidra version (11.3.2 -> release 2025.06.28 assets; 12.x -> release 2026.06.04 assets) -> File -> Install Extensions -> '+' -> zip -> restart Ghidra.",
          "Install GhidraMCP: (11.3.2) GhidraMCP-release-1-4.zip via Install Extensions; (12.x) build fork or LaurieWired source with 'mvn clean package assembly:single' after copying the 8 Ghidra jars into lib/. Restart, then File -> Configure -> Developer -> check GhidraMCPPlugin; confirm port 8080 under Edit -> Tool Options -> GhidraMCP HTTP Server.",
          "Extract the main PSX-EXE (SLUS_007.26) from the BFM disc image and import into Ghidra — the PSX loader is auto-selected ('PSX Executables Loader', language PSX:LE:32:default); accept and run auto-analysis with the 'PsyQ Signatures' analyzer enabled.",
          "After analysis, read the detected PSY-Q SDK version at Edit -> Options for Program -> Program Information -> 'PsyQ Version' (append '.0' if a \"'psyq/xx' cannot be found\" error appears); record it — it pins the compiler/library versions for the matching build later.",
          "Set up the MCP bridge: pip install mcp requests; add project .mcp.json pointing Claude Code at python bridge_mcp_ghidra.py --ghidra-server http://127.0.0.1:8080/ (stdio).",
          "Operating procedure each session: start Ghidra, open the BFM program in CodeBrowser FIRST, then start Claude Code; verify connectivity with a cheap call like list_segments or get_current_address before heavier work.",
          "Only if migrating a pre-existing non-PSX Ghidra project: set language to PSX, run CreateGteMacSegment (Script Manager -> PSX GTE), reanalyze."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "Ghidra 11.3.2 (stock-compatible with both plugins) OR Ghidra 12.0.4/12.1 (needs GhidraMCP fork/self-build) — github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/releases",
          "JDK 21 (Temurin/OpenJDK) — required by Ghidra 11.3+/12.x and by Maven builds of GhidraMCP",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr — prebuilt zip per Ghidra version from github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr/releases (11.3.2 build in tag 2025.06.28; 12.1 build in tag 2026.06.04); install via File -> Install Extensions",
          "GhidraMCP 1.4 (GhidraMCP-release-1-4.zip, Ghidra 11.3.2) from github.com/LaurieWired/GhidraMCP/releases — or fork ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12 (Ghidra 12.0.1) / bethington/ghidra-mcp (Ghidra 12.0.3, 200+ tools) for Ghidra 12.x",
          "Python 3 (native Windows) + 'pip install mcp requests' for bridge_mcp_ghidra.py",
          "Maven + JDK 21 only if rebuilding GhidraMCP for Ghidra 12.x (mvn clean package assembly:single after copying Base/Decompiler/Docking/Generic/Project/SoftwareModeling/Utility/Gui jars to lib/)",
          "psx_psyq_signatures (github.com/lab313ru/psx_psyq_signatures) — already bundled in ghidra_psx_ldr release zips; clone separately only for the signature generator or reference",
          "PSY-Q SDK .LIB/.OBJ archives (for the later matching-build phase): psx.arthus.net/sdk/ or archive.org PsyQ mirrors"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Entire Ghidra analysis stack runs natively on Windows 11: Ghidra (Java/JDK 21), ghidra_psx_ldr (pure Java extension), GhidraMCP plugin (Java) and bridge_mcp_ghidra.py (plain Python 3 + mcp + requests, stdio transport works directly with Claude Code on Windows). No WSL2 required for this topic. WSL2 would only enter the picture for the separate build/toolchain side of the decomp (PSY-Q wine wrappers, maspsx/GCC builds), not for the Ghidra/MCP stack. One Windows-specific note: in .mcp.json use double-backslash absolute paths and ensure 'python' resolves to the intended interpreter (or use the full path to python.exe / 'py').",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "Version lock conflict is the central constraint: official GhidraMCP tops out at Ghidra 11.3.2 (1.4, June 2025, repo effectively unmaintained — issues #83/#131 open), while ghidra_psx_ldr dropped 11.x after tag 2026.01.04. Stock-binary sweet spot = Ghidra 11.3.2; anything newer requires a GhidraMCP fork or self-build.",
          "Ghidra extensions are point-version-locked (extension.properties); installing a zip built for a different Ghidra point release shows red/fails to load. GhidraMCP 1.4 specifically breaks on Ghidra 11.4+ with 'Invalid line encountered: ModuleName=GhidraMCP' (Module.manifest format change, issue #83).",
          "GhidraMCP large-project limitations: list_functions has NO pagination (can return tens of thousands of lines on a full PS1 EXE — prefer list_methods with offset/limit=100); name-based tools (decompile_function, get_function_xrefs) act on first name match; no tools for struct/data-type creation, global retyping, byte reads, or byte-pattern search in 1.4; responses are plain text over HTTP with possible truncation/timeouts on big decompilations; server binds one port = one Ghidra instance/program at a time, and tools silently fail if no program is open in CodeBrowser.",
          "PsyQ signature analyzer defaults: 'Minimal signature entropy' = 3.0 can skip tiny library functions (low-entropy stubs); 'Only first match' affects duplicated code; if version detection fails, set 'PsyQ Version if not found' analyzer option or the Program Information 'PsyQ Version' field, and append '.0' to fix \"'psyq/xx' cannot be found\" errors.",
          "Building ghidra_psx_ldr from source requires git clone --recursive (data/psyq is a submodule) and the Eclipse GhidraDev workflow; prefer the prebuilt release zips.",
          "GhidraMCP 1.4's set_function_prototype/set_local_variable_type are best-effort on MIPS/PSX (custom PSX language) — verify a few edits in the GUI before trusting bulk changes; legacy projects also need CreateGteMacSegment + reanalysis or GTE macro calls will not decompile.",
          "Avoid running Ghidra auto-analysis concurrently with MCP-driven writes (renames/comments) — both mutate the program DB on the Swing/transaction model and can stall each other.",
          "Third-party GhidraMCP forks (bethington/ghidra-mcp '200+ tools', GhidraMCP-12, themixednuts/GhidraMCP) are unaudited; pin a commit and review before adopting for a long-lived project."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "Which PSY-Q SDK version Brave Fencer Musashi SLUS-00726 actually uses — will be answered by DetectPsyQ at import (Program Information -> PsyQ Version); this pins the matching toolchain later.",
          "Final stack decision: Ghidra 11.3.2 with both stock plugins vs Ghidra 12.1 with official ghidra_psx_ldr + self-built/forked GhidraMCP — who maintains the GhidraMCP build going forward?",
          "Whether GhidraMCP handles PSX overlays/GTEMAC overlay address spaces correctly (decompile_function_by_address on overlay addresses is untested territory).",
          "Whether the bundled psyq signature set for BFM's SDK version yields good coverage on SLUS-00726, or whether custom signatures from raw PSY-Q OBJ files (marker-file import path) will be needed.",
          "Whether a richer fork (bethington/ghidra-mcp with 200+ tools incl. data-type/struct ops) is worth the audit cost versus LaurieWired 1.4's 27 tools for a matching-decomp workflow that needs heavy retyping."
        ],
        "sources": [
