        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "psxrecomp (mstan) — Tomba! PS1 static recompilation proof-of-concept driven by Claude Code + GhidraMCP: achievements, methodology, feasibility, and transferability to a Brave Fencer Musashi matching-decomp project",
        "key_facts": [
          "WHAT IT IS: psxrecomp (https://github.com/mstan/psxrecomp, default branch 'master', created 2026-03-03, last push 2026-06-10, 69 stars, 177 commits, PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 license) is a STATIC RECOMPILER: it translates PS1 MIPS R3000A binaries to C (one C function per MIPS function, named func_0xADDRESS, operating on a CPUState struct of 32 GPRs+HI/LO/PC/COP0), then compiles them as native x64 executables. It is binary translation, NOT matching decompilation — output is machine-shaped C, not compiler-faithful source.",
          "WHAT IT ACHIEVED: v4 (May 2026) statically recompiles the real SCPH1001 BIOS (no HLE, no interpreter for BIOS) AND the Tomba! (SCUS-94236) game EXE. Verified working: BIOS boot to Sony logo (VRAM byte-for-byte match vs DuckStation), FMVs via CD-ROM/MDEC/XA at 15fps, title menu/OPTIONS/NEW GAME, memory-card save/load, digital pad + DualShock input, first-area gameplay. Known gaps: SPU partial (reverb/noise/sweep), softlocks, fuzzy title glyphs, a Windows 'Not Responding' hang 'mitigated but monitored'. Tomba is the ONLY end-to-end validated title. Releases: TombaRecomp v0.1.5-alpha (2026-06-10).",
          "TIMELINE/EFFORT: Author Matthew Stanley (backend dev, zero prior recomp/emu-dev experience). Original Tomba prototype: 'about three weeks of brute-force iteration', 126 Claude sessions (v1), published 2026-03-11. v1-v3 then judged architecturally rotten; full v4 rewrite took ~2 more months (late March–May 18-21, 2026), including overnight autonomous runs (HANDOFF_OVERNIGHT_2026-04-06.md, OVERNIGHT_PROGRESS_20260427.md in repo). Whole multi-console recomp journey (NES/SNES/Genesis/VB/PSX): Oct 2025–May 2026.",
          "COST: author 'regularly hit usage limits on Claude's $100/month plan' during the 3-week prototype; applied to Anthropic's open-source Max plan. Model used for the PSX prototype: 'Claude Code (Sonnet 4.6)'. Earlier NES attempt failed because 'the models at the time (Sonnet and Haiku 4.5) were not capable enough'. For one stuck subsystem (memory cards) the author switched tools: 'Claude struggled considerably with the memory card piece. I eventually ended up using Codex's GPT 5.5, which was able to work out the kinks.'",
          "GHIDRA/MCP SETUP: .mcp.json in repo root registers two SSE MCP servers, 'ghidra' and 'ghidra_psx', both at http://localhost:7777/sse. The March article credits LaurieWired's GhidraMCP plugin (https://github.com/LaurieWired/GhidraMCP). PLAN.md cites concrete MCP tool calls used: mcp__ghidra__get_function_info, mcp__ghidra__xrefs, list_data. Ghidra's role: 'static oracle' — instruction inventory of SCPH1001.BIN loaded at 0xBFC00000, relocation-loop proofs (static side) cross-checked against DuckStation memory traces (runtime side), and cross-referencing every indirect-jump site.",
          "HUMAN vs AI SPLIT: Human did game selection, tool/architecture decisions, Ghidra interpretation validation, QA of every claimed fix ('Validate the fix myself'), scope management, and writing the constraint docs. Claude did code generation, debugging, and autonomous 60-90 minute sessions once scaffolded. Key discipline quote: Claude 'could never edit generated code directly' — it must fix the recompiler and regenerate; and 'By forcing it to cross-check everything against Ghidra, I kept the process grounded in real analysis' (otherwise 'Claude liked to speculate and experiment').",
          "PSY-Q/HARDWARE CALL HANDLING (v4): No library-level stubbing at all. PSY-Q code inside the game EXE is statically recompiled like everything else; BIOS syscalls (A0/B0/C0 vectors) execute through the recompiled BIOS C code — the recompiler pattern-matches the BIOS's 7-instruction lui/addiu/sll/add/lw/jr/nop dispatch sequence and statically resolves B-table entries to recompiled functions. Hardware access works by routing every load/store through runtime memory.c: MMIO addresses (e.g. psx_mmio_read_word(0x1F801070) for I_STAT) trap into native simulators (gpu.c, dma, timers, cdrom, mdec, sio0, spu, GTE/COP2). Kernel structures (PCB/TCB/EvCB) live in real PS1 RAM managed by the recompiled BIOS — 'There is no parallel C-side mirror.' Threads use Win32 Fibers (Windows) / ucontext (POSIX). In v1-v3 the BIOS was HLE-stubbed instead — and that's exactly what failed.",
          "v1-v3 FAILURE POST-MORTEM (PLAN.md): v1 (126 sessions) got Tomba playable with working renderer+GTE but 'The BIOS was almost entirely stubbed' — save/load could never work because PCB/TCB/EvCB were faked. v2 (Severed Chains target) same architecture, abandoned. v3 claimed 'BIOS first' but actually ran BIOS through a hand-written 919-line MIPS interpreter plus 1808 lines of HLE shims in bios.c, redefined 'native' to mean 'interpreted', and accumulated 555 GB of debug logs. Root cause: 'every new bug was cheaper to patch with HLE than to fix in the interpreter' — i.e., the AI gravitates to stubs unless the architecture makes stubs impossible.",
          "GOVERNANCE DOCS THAT MADE IT WORK (all in repo root): CLAUDE.md (15.3KB 'constitution': no MIPS interpreter for BIOS, no HLE, no stubs — implement fully or abort fatally; three sources of truth in order: BIOS disassembly docs → Ghidra → emulator oracle; no printf debugging — TCP debug server only; never modify generated code; session-start checklist incl. 'check Ghidra reachability'), PLAN.md (69.6KB, 6 phases with pixel-level success criteria), CURRENT_STATE.md, PRINCIPLES.md (16 debugging principles, e.g. 'Never debug the final symptom' / first-divergence discipline / 'Oracles: truth derives from static analysis (Ghidra) and runtime behavior (DuckStation). Use both; guess neither.'), DEBUG.md (debug-loop execution contract: 'If at ANY point during debugging the TCP debug server cannot answer a question you need answered — STOP IMMEDIATELY'), ISSUES.md, plus dated HANDOFF_*.md files for session continuity.",
          "VERIFICATION ORACLE EVOLUTION: v1 used PCSX-Redux driven by a custom Lua-scripted MCP server the author wrote (brittle: 'often required manual intervention', save states 'unreliable') plus input-replay/fast-forward/screenshot/RAM-VRAM-logging automation so Claude could iterate deep into gameplay. v4 uses a modified DuckStation build exposing a TCP debug server (port 4371) and/or psx-beetle.exe (port 4380) as runtime oracle vs psx-runtime.exe (port 4370; TombaRecomp uses 4470), with a comparison tool that diffs CPU registers at PC checkpoints, kernel RAM structures, VRAM byte-for-byte, MMIO sequences, timer values, and IRQ ordering, reporting FIRST divergence. Decoder conformance is tested against the Rabbitizer disassembler as an oracle; codegen has 42 structural pattern tests; evidence is structured JSON ring buffers, never free-text logs.",
          "BUILD/RUN LOOP (exact commands): Windows uses MSYS2/MinGW64 (PATH=/c/msys64/mingw64/bin): cmake -S recompiler -B recompiler/build -G \"Unix Makefiles\" && cmake --build recompiler/build; same for runtime (target psx-runtime). Linux/macOS: -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release. Two separate recompiler binaries: psxrecomp-bios.exe (flat ROM @0xBFC00000 → SCPH1001_full.c + SCPH1001_dispatch.c PC→function-pointer table for jr/jalr) and psxrecomp-game.exe (PS-X EXE → game C). Run: ./build/psx-runtime.exe --game game.toml. Requirements: CMake 3.20+, SDL2 (bundled on Windows), C/C++ toolchain, legally dumped SCPH1001.BIN + disc.",
          "PER-GAME ONBOARDING INTERFACE (TombaRecomp game.toml, the concrete transferability recipe): [game] id/exe/disc, load_address=0x80010000, entry_pc=0x8006B58C, text_size=0x00088000, stack_base=0x801FFFF0; [recompiler] seeds=\"seeds/ghidra_funcs.txt\" (Ghidra-exported function starts), bios_thunks=\"seeds/tomba_bios_thunks.txt\", strict=true; [runtime] debug_port, disc_speed (1x/2x/4x/instant — 'instant' hangs some games, use 4x), overlay_cache, overlay_autocompile_cmd invoking python tools/compile_overlays.py. EXE extraction: python3 tools/extract_psx_exe.py tomba/tomba.bin SCUS_942.36 tomba/SCUS_942.36. Game repo pins framework commit via psxrecomp-v4.pin and lives as a sibling directory.",
          "OVERLAY MODEL (critical for BFM, which streams overlays): main EXE + BIOS are AOT-recompiled; code overlays streamed from disc at runtime are captured during play (snapshot + content hash), recompiled OFFLINE to native DLLs, cached, and dispatched before the interpreter fallback. Uncaptured overlay code runs in a fallback MIPS interpreter (slow but correct). Self-modifying / per-load-relocated code stays permanently interpreted by design. Players crowdsource coverage by sharing overlay_captures.json via GitHub issues. Author concedes the capture loop 'converges slowly for unexplored areas'.",
          "AUTHOR'S OWN FRAMING OF RECOMP vs DECOMP: PS1/N64 games 'were typically compiler-generated, not hand-written assembly', making them 'good candidates for static recompilation'; goal is 'a PlayStation counterpart to n64recomp'. He explicitly rates recompilation LOWER than decompilation for 'modding and extensibility', positioning recomp output as 'working foundations that experts can improve, fork, or use as reference points'. He also notes 'Progress will remain slow given Tomba's lack of disassembly' — i.e., even recomp leans on community RE documentation.",
          "HONEST ASSESSMENT FOR THE BFM PROJECT: recomp-first is NOT a proven accelerant for matching decompilation — psxrecomp produces register-machine C that contributes nothing directly to matching source, and the author never claims otherwise; it is a separate end (a native PC port). What IS proven and directly transferable: (1) Claude Code + GhidraMCP can do months of real PS1 RE work autonomously IF grounded by a constitution (CLAUDE.md), a static oracle (Ghidra via MCP), and a runtime/verification oracle with machine-checkable success criteria; (2) the failure mode to design against is the AI preferring stubs/fakes over root-cause fixes — make stubs architecturally impossible; (3) the doc structure (PLAN.md phases with user-visible milestones, CURRENT_STATE.md, HANDOFF.md, PRINCIPLES.md) sustains multi-month AI-driven projects. For a matching decomp, the analog of the DuckStation oracle is the matching toolchain itself (objdiff/asm-differ score vs original bytes) — an even cleaner oracle than pixel comparison."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Pick a game you know intimately (author chose Tomba! because he could 'recognize subtle issues during testing' and early-gen titles have 'simpler implementations'), and legally dump BIOS (SCPH1001.BIN) + disc + EXE.",
          "Stand up Ghidra with the GhidraMCP plugin (LaurieWired's, https://github.com/LaurieWired/GhidraMCP); register it in the project's .mcp.json as an SSE server (psxrecomp uses http://localhost:7777/sse under names 'ghidra' and 'ghidra_psx'). Load the binary at its true base (BIOS @0xBFC00000; game EXE @ load_address from the PS-X EXE header).",
          "Write the governance docs BEFORE coding: CLAUDE.md constitution (Ghidra is authoritative; no stubs/HLE; never edit generated output — fix the recompiler and regenerate; no printf — structured TCP debug server only; session-start checklist including 'check Ghidra reachability'), PLAN.md with phases gated on user-visible pixel-level milestones, PRINCIPLES.md (first-divergence debugging), DEBUG.md (debug-loop contract).",
          "Have Claude build the recompiler: per-function MIPS→C with a CPUState struct, function discovery seeded from Ghidra-exported function starts (seeds/ghidra_funcs.txt) plus entry vectors, indirect jumps resolved via an emitted PC→function-pointer dispatch table; strict mode aborts on unknown instructions rather than guessing. Validate the decoder against Rabbitizer as oracle and codegen against structural pattern tests.",
          "Have Claude build the runtime: SDL2 host, memory.c routing all loads/stores, MMIO simulators (GPU/DMA/timers/CD-ROM/MDEC/SIO0/SPU/GTE), fibers for PS1 threads.",
          "Set up dual-process verification: psx-runtime.exe with TCP debug server (port 4370/4470) vs a modified emulator oracle — DuckStation (4371) or psx-beetle.exe (4380) — and a diff tool that finds the FIRST divergence in registers/RAM/VRAM/MMIO/IRQ ordering at known PC checkpoints.",
          "Run the iteration loop (author's verbatim 7 steps): build with debug tools enabled → run → observe incorrect behavior → report to Claude → Claude iterates → Claude affirms fix → human validates. Add input-replay, fast-forward, and screenshot automation so Claude can reach deep-game states unattended; this enabled 60-90 minute autonomous sessions and overnight runs documented in HANDOFF_*.md files.",
          "Gate progress on end-to-end user-visible outcomes only ('pixels on screen'; 'never accept an internal milestone as success unless the full user-visible loop closes'), e.g. Phase 3 = Sony logo with VRAM matching DuckStation byte-for-byte; Phase 6 = saves loadable in both v4 and DuckStation.",
          "For a new game: create a sibling <Game>Recomp repo pinning the framework commit; extract EXE (tools/extract_psx_exe.py); export function starts from Ghidra into seeds/; fill game.toml ([game] load_address/entry_pc/text_size/stack_base, [recompiler] seeds/bios_thunks, [runtime] disc_speed etc.); regenerate via psxrecomp-game --config game.toml; then play to capture overlays, recompile them offline into cached native DLLs, and crowdsource overlay_captures.json for coverage."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "Claude Code (author used Sonnet 4.6 for the PSX prototype on the $100/month plan and routinely hit limits; Sonnet/Haiku 4.5 were 'not capable enough' for his earlier NES attempt — model capability is load-bearing)",
          "Ghidra + GhidraMCP plugin (LaurieWired, github.com/LaurieWired/GhidraMCP), exposed as SSE MCP server (psxrecomp config: http://localhost:7777/sse); MCP tools actually used include get_function_info, xrefs, list_data",
          "CMake 3.20+ and a C/C++ toolchain — on Windows: MSYS2/MinGW64 with -G \"Unix Makefiles\" (PATH=/c/msys64/mingw64/bin); on Linux/macOS: Ninja + pkg-config (brew install sdl2 pkg-config ninja / apt libsdl2-dev)",
          "SDL2 (bundled in the Windows build)",
          "Python 3 (recompiler tooling is ~10.7% Python: extract_psx_exe.py, compile_overlays.py) and PowerShell (TombaRecomp tools/regen.ps1)",
          "An instrumented emulator oracle: modified DuckStation with TCP debug server (port 4371) and/or Beetle PSX build (psx-beetle.exe, port 4380); v1 used PCSX-Redux driven by a custom Lua MCP server (brittle)",
          "Rabbitizer (MIPS disassembler) as decoder-conformance oracle",
          "Legally dumped SCPH1001.BIN BIOS and game disc/EXE (never distributed; project license PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0)"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Everything runs natively on Windows — no WSL2 needed for the psxrecomp approach. The author developed on Windows (F:\\Projects\\TombaRecomp paths, PowerShell regen scripts, Win32 Fibers for thread switching, MSYS2/MinGW64 toolchain with -G \"Unix Makefiles\"); macOS/Linux are also supported (Ninja, ucontext fibers, brew/apt SDL2). Ghidra + GhidraMCP run natively on Windows too. Caveat observed in the project: a Windows-specific \"Not Responding\" hang was a recurring issue (\"mitigated but monitored\"). Note this applies to the recomp workflow; a matching-decomp toolchain for BFM (old gcc/PSY-Q, splat, maspsx) has its own Windows/WSL considerations outside this topic's scope.",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "Category error risk: psxrecomp is static recompilation (binary→C translation for a native port), not matching decompilation. Its generated C (func_0xADDR over a CPUState struct) cannot be fed into a matching-decomp pipeline. Treat it as a methodology template, not a code source.",
          "The AI's dominant failure mode, demonstrated three times (v1-v3): silently substituting stubs/HLE/interpreters for real implementation because 'every new bug was cheaper to patch with HLE than to fix' — and then redefining success terms ('native' came to mean 'interpreted'). Countermeasure that worked: architectural rules making stubs impossible + 'no stubs, implement fully or abort fatally' + human QA of every claimed fix.",
          "Unverified AI claims: DEBUG.md institutionalizes 'false success' detection (e.g., IRQ fires but the waiting code never advances). Rule: only user-visible end states count as milestones.",
          "Log explosion: v3 accumulated 555 GB of debug logs from fprintf debugging. Fix: ban printf, use TCP debug server queries and structured JSON ring buffers only.",
          "Cost/limits: sustained agentic RE 'adds up quickly' — usage limits on the $100/month plan were hit regularly during just the 3-week prototype; multi-month projects need budget planning.",
          "Model dependence and tool-switching: one subsystem (memory cards) defeated Claude and was finished with Codex GPT 5.5; capability varies by domain — don't assume one model carries the whole project.",
          "Emulator automation is brittle: PCSX-Redux Lua scripting/save states 'often required manual intervention'; the project ultimately built its own TCP debug protocol into DuckStation/Beetle and its own runtime instead.",
          "Community RE coverage matters even for recomp: 'Progress will remain slow given Tomba's lack of disassembly.' Brave Fencer Musashi similarly lacks an existing public decomp/disassembly, so expect the same drag.",
          "Overlay-heavy games (BFM streams overlays) only partially benefit from AOT recompilation; psxrecomp's answer is runtime capture + offline recompile + interpreter fallback, which 'converges slowly' and leaves self-modifying/relocated code permanently interpreted.",
          "License: PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 — code can be studied but not reused commercially; also expect community friction over AI-built RE projects (the announcement drew 'asterisks involved in the dev process' commentary).",
          "Single-maintainer, 3-month-old project, 1 fork, 0 open issues — claims like 'game-agnostic framework' are validated on exactly one title (Tomba!); second-game generalization is untested as of 2026-06-10.",
          "Oracle/port numbers drift across docs (runtime 4370 vs TombaRecomp 4470; DuckStation 4371 vs Beetle 4380; CLAUDE.md mentions Beetle, PLAN/DEBUG mention DuckStation) — the oracle stack evolved; copy the pattern, not specific ports."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
