      {
        "topic": "Existing reverse-engineering knowledge about Brave Fencer Musashi (PS1, SLUS-00726 USA): TCRF debug menu/unused content/regional diffs, AiroRom LZSS documentation, .CD container, RAM map, prototypes, and tooling",
        "key_facts": [
          "TCRF debug menu (source: Squaresoft74): enabled via GameShark, then press L3 in-game. USA code: 'D0078DC2 0200' + '800B99DE 0007'; Japan: 'D0077F22 0200' + '800B8B3E 0007'; Japan Demo: 'D0077D4A 0200' + '800B894E 0007'. Navigate with D-pad, Circle to accept. TCRF states only the area/room selection submenu works; everything else in the menu is non-functional. Interpretation: 0x80078DC2 (US) is a raw pad halfword (0x0200 = L3), and 0x800B99DE (US) is the game-mode/state variable -- writing 7 switches into debug-menu mode. This is a strong code-entry anchor for Ghidra.",
          "TCRF unused content: only one item documented -- music track 128 'Burning Lava and Twisting Steel', never used, themed like the restaurant-basement-ruins tracks. No unused graphics/areas are documented on TCRF.",
          "TCRF regional differences (JP->US localization): alcohol censorship -- Thirstquencher Empire was ル・コーアル帝国 (anagram of アルコール 'alcohol'); character renames: Tequila->Flatski, Brandy->Gingerelle, Liqueur->Bubbles, Bordeaux->Rootrick, Jean Vodka->Jon Capricciola; drunk restaurant-owner dialogue rewritten to 'soda pop/caffeine'. Sword renames: らいこうまる->Fusion (ability ゲット・イン->'Assimilation'), レイガンド (Reigando)->Lumina. No PAL/Europe release exists.",
          "AiroRom (github.com/pleonex/AiroRom) is actually an unrelated NDS data-protection thesis; its ONLY BFM content is one wiki page 'Brave fencer Musashi' containing a fully annotated MIPS R3000 disassembly of the game's LZSS text decompressor. Fetch it raw at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wiki/pleonex/AiroRom/Brave-fencer-Musashi.md (the GitHub wiki UI fails to render). The repo's BinMesG tool is for Ninokuni BMG files, NOT BFM.",
          "AiroRom LZSS decompressor details (VERIFIED byte-for-byte against local SLUS-00726 EXE): routine at 0x80018730; 1024-byte (2^10) ring buffer in PS1 scratchpad at 0x1F800000; token/flag byte processed with mask starting 0x01 shifting left to 0x80; flag bit 1 = literal byte (copied to output AND ring buffer); flag bit 0 = 16-bit little-endian code where low 10 bits = ABSOLUTE ring-buffer index (not a relative distance) and high 6 bits + 2 = copy length; a code whose low 10 bits are 0 terminates the stream. Input is consumed in 0x800-byte (CD-sector) chunks; the routine is a resumable state machine.",
          "LZSS decompressor state machine (US addresses, locally verified): entry-point table at 0x80072A30 (bytes 00000000 FFFFFFFF then 5 pointers): state 0 -> 0x80018824 (finish), 1 -> 0x80018790 (init token ring), 2 -> 0x800187A4 (check token bit), 3 -> 0x800187F8 (process match), 4 -> 0x800188B4 (next token bit). State variable at 0x800C7D24. Persistent state: 0x800747A0 current mask (byte), 0x800747A4 current token byte, 0x800747AC output pointer, 0x800747B0 ring-buffer write index, 0x800747B4 partial 16-bit match code. Routine returns at 0x80018910.",
          "Local disc verification (Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp\\Brave Fencer Musashi (USA)\\, redump 4-track image: Track 1 data + 3 CDDA): ISO9660 volume 'MUSASHI'; SYSTEM.CNF = 'BOOT = cdrom:\\SLUS_007.26;1 / TCB = 4 / EVENT = 16 / STACK = 801ffff0'; PS-X EXE header: pc0=0x80010000, t_addr=0x80010000, t_size=0x64800 (so EXE spans 0x80010000-0x80074800; the 0x800747xx LZSS state vars sit in its BSS), sp=0x801FFFF0; region string 'Sony Computer Entertainment Inc. for North America area'.",
          "Full disc file list (LBA, size): SLUS_007.26 (24, 413696), MUSA.ID (226, 9 bytes 'MUSASHI\\r\\n'), LIST.CD (227, 4096), MAIN.CD (229, 5988352), SC01.CD (6161, 20078592), SC02.CD (33309, 22126592), SC03.CD (54577, 64569344), SC04.CD (94617, 19425280), SC05.CD (112806, 16498688), SC06.CD (125374, 23928832), SC07.CD (146146, 12734464) -- the .CD files are the game's data containers (MAIN + 7 per-chapter archives). STR FMVs: LOGOA/LOGOB/OPEN/END/ST01-ST06/SHOPS01/OUT; DA audio: DUMMY_DA.DA (28.5 MB padding), ST01_13A.DA, ST01_13B.DA.",
          ".CD container format (observed locally, arithmetic verified): header = u32 sub-file count (MAIN.CD: 0x31 = 49), u32 zero, then per-entry {u32 start_offset_in_2048B_sectors, u32 size_in_bytes}; entries are sector-aligned and contiguous. LIST.CD's first bytes are IDENTICAL to MAIN.CD's sector 0, i.e. LIST.CD is a standalone copy of (at least) MAIN.CD's TOC, presumably read by the EXE; LIST.CD is 4096 bytes (2 sectors) so it may also hold TOC data for SC0x.CD -- unconfirmed.",
          "Existing .CD extraction tool: 'Brave Fencer extractor' (BRAVE) v1.0 by CUE, released 16 Mar 2013 on romhacking.net utility #939, Windows CLI, usage 'BRAVE file [file ...]' on .CD files, C SOURCE INCLUDED (the reference implementation of the LZSS variant). Page: https://www.romhacking.net/utilities/939/ (site Cloudflare-blocks bots; readable via Wayback snapshot 20230319065335; download path /download/utilities/939/).",
          "NTSC-U RAM map from GameShark codes (gamehacking.org/game/88529): 0x80078E8E money(?), 0x80078EB2/0x80078EB4 HP cur/max, 0x80078EB6/0x80078EB8 BP cur/max, 0x80078EE4 Body level, 0x80078EEC Mind level, 0x80078EF0 Mind exp, 0x80078EF4 Fusion(FUS) level, 0x80078EF8 FUS exp, 0x80078EFC Lumina(LUM) level, 0x80078F00 LUM exp, 0x80078F08 exp/quick-level, 0x800AE640 music modifier (byte), 0x800AE658 all-techs bitfield, 0x800B9A08 map/location modifier, 0x800BA286 raft logs, 0x80078DCA joker/pad mirror, 0x80126B8E moon-jump (player Y-vel area), 0x80126D98 midair-jump counter, 0x800AFAFB/0x800AFAFD screen tilt bytes, 0x800AFB0C model weirdness, 0x800A63F3 shading. A coherent player/stats block lives at 0x80078Dxx-0x80078Fxx and flags/inventory at 0x800AE6xx.",
          "Speedrun autosplitter (LiveSplit registry -> gist by Aalaizah, BizHawk octoshock.dll offsets; PS1 address = offset - 0x30DF90 + 0x80000000, conversion cross-verified via playerLocation): progress/story counter u32 at 0x80078E80; playerLocation s16 at 0x800B9A08 (start trigger value 12293; other room IDs: 4113, 12325, 8283, 12340/12345/12353/12350, 12354, 12321, 12291, 12380, 12389, 12391, 12405, 12417, 12435, 12432, 12424, 12427, 12431, 12448); chests byte 0x800AE652; armor 0x800AE64B; boss HP: SteamKnight 0x801E4398, Skullpion/QueenAnt/TowerOfDeath/DarkLumina2 0x80120316, RelicKeeper 0x8018EE00, Kojiro 0x801212CA, FrostDragon 0x801EFD28, Ben/Ed 0x8012052E, DarkLumina finale 0x801F8714. Boss HP living at 0x8012xxxx/0x801Exxxx implies overlay/entity code+data loaded high in RAM.",
          "Archipelago randomizer repo github.com/AegeusEvander/Brave-Fencer-Musashi-AP-World (active, updated May 2026) is the richest living RAM map, covering BOTH US and JP builds with constant deltas: US->JP main state block -0xEA0 (menu state 0x0B99DE US <-> 0x0B8B3E JP -- independently consistent with TCRF's per-version debug codes), zone-loaded block -0xE70, stats table -0xEA4, code regions vary (+0x2A8, +0xA70, +0x344, +0x2D0, +0xE8). Key MAIN_RAM offsets (add 0x80000000): 0x0B99DE menu/game-mode, 0x0B9A08 current location u16, 0x078EB4 current HP, 0x0AE64A/0x0AE64B scrolls + legendary armor, 0x0AE650 Minku flags, 0x0AE651 chest flags (5B), 0x0AE666 castle-guard state, 0x0AE671 Bincho-rescue flags (9B), 0x0BA1E7 inventory, 0x0BA1F3 geezer, 0x0BA21B toy collection (43B), 0x0BA285 Steamwood status, 0x075400 zone-loaded + destination list, 0x0638FA + 16*level = body-stat level table, 0x00BA94 build-identifier bytes. Also: jp_encoding.py documents the JP custom font encoding (digits 0x20-0x29, hiragana sequential from あ=0x30, 0xF0-prefixed two-byte codes for some glyphs); client patches game live via BizHawk (MAIN_RAM and GPURAM writes -- hair color edited directly in VRAM at GPURAM 0x86AC4).",
          "Prototypes (Hidden Palace 'Project Deluge', released Apr 17 2021, both emulatable, no debug symbols noted): (1) 'Musashi Demo', build Aug 31 1998 12:56:08 -- pressing Start on the title screen opens a SCENE/AREA SELECTION menu instead of the main menu (likely the same code behind the retail L3 debug menu), fewer voice lines; download https://archive.org/details/BraveFencerMusashiAug311998prototype (img CRC32 1369DE07, 60.7 MB). (2) 'Musashi Master', build Sep 8 1998 12:00:00 -- late US localization master, near-final ('Dual Shock Vibration' vs final 'Vibration', one dialog line changed); download https://archive.org/details/BraveFencerMusashiSep81998prototype (img CRC32 5C24728E, 416 MB). Hidden Palace lists US final build date Sep 22 1998, US release Oct 31 1998 (TCRF infobox says Nov 12 1998 citing a Squaresoft press release -- unresolved discrepancy).",
          "Versions/serials: USA SLUS-00726 (psxdatacenter also lists demo disc SLUS-90029 with the entry); Japan SLPS-01490 'Brave Fencer Musashiden' (released Jul 16 1998); Japan re-release SLPS-02769 (Square Millennium Collection). No PAL version. Per AiroRom, several fan-translation attempts historically failed because the compressed text could not be located -- the LZSS doc + CUE's BRAVE tool solved this.",
          "No existing decompilation project: GitHub search (Jun 2026) finds only the Archipelago AP-world, its PopTracker pack (Aalaizah/FencerPop), and an unrelated Unreal fan project. No public engine documentation, no save/memory-card format documentation found anywhere."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Seed Ghidra with verified anchors: LZSS decompressor 0x80018730, jump table 0x80072A30, state var 0x800C7D24, state block 0x800747A0-0x800747B4, ring buffer 0x1F800000 (scratchpad), pad halfwords 0x80078DC2/0x80078DCA, game-mode var 0x800B99DE (7 = debug menu), location id 0x800B9A08, player stats block 0x80078E80-0x80078F08, flags block 0x800AE640-0x800AE671, story counter 0x80078E80.",
          "Obtain CUE's BRAVE extractor v1.0 (romhacking.net utility 939 via Wayback) and use its C source as the reference LZSS implementation; extract LIST.CD/MAIN.CD/SC01-07.CD sub-files before any asset analysis.",
          "Cross-reference the .CD TOC reader in the EXE: find code referencing LBA 227 (LIST.CD) / the {sector,size} pair format observed locally to map sub-file -> RAM load address (overlay map).",
          "Trace the debug menu: set a breakpoint/xref on writes of 7 to 0x800B99DE and on the L3 bit test of 0x80078DC2 to locate the menu dispatch table; compare with the Aug 31 1998 prototype where the scene-select menu is reachable from Start on title.",
          "Mine the Archipelago repo (client.py, progression_state.py, locations.py, dialog_locations.py, jp_encoding.py, store_info.py) for additional labeled addresses and the JP<->US deltas when naming symbols.",
          "Download both Hidden Palace prototypes from archive.org for diffing against retail (earlier build = more debug pathways; Sep 8 master nearly matches retail and can validate tooling)."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
