      },
      {
        "topic": "General PS1 matching-decompilation process: canonical tooling (splat/maspsx/old-gcc), templates, PCSX-Redux role, Ghidra integration, and CI/progress tracking — to cross-check the sotn-specific picture for a Brave Fencer Musashi (SLUS-00726) decomp",
        "key_facts": [
          "No official 'psx-decomp-template' exists. The community-canonical starting points are: (a) `splat create_config <SLUS exe>` to auto-generate a base YAML, then copy Makefile conventions from reference repos; (b) https://github.com/alam0rt/nix-decomp-psx — a Nix-flake template explicitly 'based on conventions established by sotn-decomp, open-ribbon, and other PSX decompilation efforts' (provides MIPS binutils+GCC, splat/spimdisasm/rabbitizer, submodules for asm-differ/m2c/maspsx/decomp-permuter, standard layout: config/ src/ include/ asm/ disks/us/ tools/ Makefile); (c) https://github.com/mkst/esa (Evo's Space Adventures) — self-described 'proof of concept, matching PSX decompilation, leveraging tools from N64 decomp projects', the original modern-toolchain PSX exemplar (`make extract`, `make all --jobs`).",
          "splat is now the pip package `splat64`: install with `python3 -m pip install -U splat64[mips]` (requirements pin used in the wild: `splat64[mips]>=0.41.0,<1.0.0`). The `[mips]` extra is required for PSX. Docs: https://github.com/ethteck/splat/wiki (Examples page lists PSX projects with compiler used).",
          "splat PSX config: `create_config.py` detects the 8-byte magic `PS-X EXE`, then emits YAML with `platform: psx`, `compiler: PSYQ`, `gp_value: 0x{initial_gp:08X}` read from the EXE header, and segments `- name: header / type: header / start: 0x0` followed by `- name: main / type: code / start: 0x800 / vram: 0x{destination_vram:X}` (vram typically 0x80010000). Command: `python3 -m splat create_config disks/us/SLUS_007.26`. Key options fields: platform, target_path, base_path, build_path (default build), src_path, asm_path, asset_path, symbol_addrs_path (symbol_addrs.txt), undefined_funcs_auto_path, reloc_addrs_path, ld_script_path (default {basename}.ld), find_file_boundaries, use_legacy_include_asm (default False), generate_asm_macros_files (default True), gp_value; PSX default add_set_gp_64=False. Segment types: header, code (with c/asm/data/rodata/bss subsegments), bin.",
          "Canonical modern PS1 build pipeline (no DOS/Wine): old GNU GCC (mipsel) -> maspsx -> GNU `as` (mipsel-linux-gnu-as) -> mipsel-linux-gnu-ld with splat-generated .ld -> objcopy to PS-EXE -> sha1 compare against original. Old compilers come prebuilt from https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc GitHub Releases (versions: 2.5.7, 2.6.0, 2.6.3, 2.7.0, 2.7.1, 2.7.2, 2.7.2.1, 2.7.2.2, 2.7.2.3, 2.8.0, 2.8.1, 2.91.66, 2.95.2; all target mipsel; 2.7.0+ accept -meb; build-it-yourself: `VERSION=2.7.2.2 make`).",
          "maspsx (https://github.com/mkst/maspsx) replaces ASPSX.EXE+psyq-obj-parser to make byte-perfect ELF objects from old-gcc assembly output. Example: `maspsx input.s --aspsx-version=2.78 --run-assembler`. Key flags: --aspsx-version=X.XX (emulates specific ASPSX nop/$gp behavior), --run-assembler (invokes mipsel-linux-gnu-as), --gnu-as-path, --dont-force-G0 (by default maspsx forces -G0 to GNU as), --expand-div (expands div/divu/rem/remu), --macro-inc, --use-comm-section, -G8 (non-zero $gp). Version behavior matrix: 1.05-2.34 use `tge` in div + nop before $at expansion; 2.30+ support -0 partial div expansion; 2.67+ support %hi/%lo; 2.77+ use $gp for symbol+offset; 2.86+ use $gp for `la`. Also supports gcc's -gcoff for line numbers in asm-differ.",
          "decomp.me PSX compiler presets (from backend coreapp/compilers.py): ids `gcc2.6.0-psx`, `gcc2.6.3-psx`, `gcc2.7.2-psx` (all use a PS1_GCC command: `/usr/bin/cpp -E -lang-c -nostdinc \"$INPUT\" -o \"$INPUT\".i && gcc $COMPILER_FLAGS -c -pipe -B$COMPILER_DIR/ -o \"$OUTPUT\" \"$INPUT\".i`), plus original-toolchain presets psyq3.5, psyq3.6, psyq4.0 (run CC1PSX.EXE/ASPSX.EXE via DOSEMU + psyq-obj-parser). PS1 flag checkboxes (COMMON_GCC_PS1_FLAGS): -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os, -g0..-g3, -fsigned-char/-funsigned-char, -G0/-G4/-G8, -mel/-meb. The near-universal matching starting point is `-O2 -G0`.",
          "Compiler-era mapping from splat wiki Examples (PSX projects): FF7 (Square, 1997) = psyq <=4.1 (gcc 2.7.2), Legacy of Kain: Soul Reaver = psyq 4.3 (gcc 2.8.1) at https://github.com/FedericoMilesi/soul-re, Evo's Space Adventures = psyq 4.6 (gcc 2.95) at https://github.com/mkst/esa. GCC 2.95.2 produces nearly identical code to PSY-Q 4.6's CC1PSX.EXE. sotn-decomp (SOTN, 1997) is the flagship gcc-2.6.3-era + maspsx project and the main reference for overlay-heavy splat configs.",
          "Repo-structure norms across PS1 decomps (open-ribbon/sotn/esa/nix template): config/ (splat.<ver>.yaml + symbols.<ver>.txt), src/ (matched C), include/, asm/ (splat-generated, gitignored), disks/ or bin/ (original game files, never committed; open-ribbon CI decrypts them with AES-256-CBC from secrets), tools/ (submodules: asm-differ, maspsx, m2c, decomp-permuter), Makefile with targets `make extract` (splat split), `make` (build+verify), `make clean`, plus diff_settings.py at root for asm-differ. MGS (https://github.com/FoxdieTeam/mgs_reversing) is the counter-example: original PSYQ SDK toolchain (separate https://github.com/FoxdieTeam/psyq_sdk repo), `python3 build.py`, native on Windows, Wine on Linux/macOS, byte-matching hash check at end, asm/ + source/ layout, progress tracked as checkmarks in README per overlay.",
          "PCSX-Redux (https://github.com/grumpycoders/pcsx-redux) runs natively on Windows x64 — nightly builds at https://distrib.app/pub/org/pcsx-redux/project/dev-win-x64; docs at https://pcsx-redux.consoledev.net. RE features: fully featured MIPS debugger, VRAM viewer/debugger, SPU debugger, Lua scripting (LuaJIT 2.1.0-beta3, Lua 5.2 compat, FFI direct memory access; console+editor under Debug menu; editor autosaves to pcsx.lua), GDB server on port 3333 (enable via Configuration > Emulation > Enable GDB server; connect `target remote localhost:3333`), web server on localhost:8080 with REST API (e.g. dump 2MB RAM: http://localhost:8080/api/v1/cpu/ram/raw). Includes OpenBIOS (boots games without retail BIOS).",
          "Ghidra integration: (1) static — ghidra_psx_ldr plugin (https://github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr) loads PS-EXE, sets MIPS 32-bit little-endian (R3000), auto-identifies PsyQ devkit library functions; install zip via File > Install Extensions. (2) live — Ghidra 10.3+ debugger over GDB: connector 'gdb', launch `<gdb-multiarch> -i mi2`, then `target remote localhost:3333` against PCSX-Redux started with `-interpreter -debugger -gdb`; Ghidra <10.3.3 requires editing MIPS processor def from mips:4000 to mips:3000. PSX RAM at 0x80000000 mirrors physical 0x0 (0x800425d0 == 0x000425d0).",
          "Progress tracking: frogress (https://github.com/decompals/frogress) is the community API, hosted at https://progress.deco.mp (endpoints /projects/ and /data/; hierarchy project>version>category>entry>measure, entries tied to git hashes). Onboarding requires an admin (ethteck on Discord/GitHub) to create a project slug + API key; upload via cli.py (cli.ini config), direct HTTP, or Python client. decomp.dev is a separate aggregator dashboard (PS1/PS2/PSP/N64/GC/etc., shows matched % + fully-linked % per commit); its onboarding mechanism is not documented on the site itself. Smaller projects skip both: open-ribbon's report.yml workflow runs `python tools/report_progress.py /src/game MAIN_T.EXE > gh-report/assets/progress-sces028.json` and commits the JSON for a README badge.",
          "Typical CI (open-ribbon build.yaml): runs-on ubuntu-20.04; `sudo apt-get install -y gcc-mips-linux-gnu`; `python3 -m pip install -r tools/splat/requirements.txt`; `make extract`; `make`; original binaries decrypted from repo secrets (AES-256-CBC) rather than committed.",
          "No existing public Brave Fencer Musashi decompilation project was found on GitHub (searched June 2026) — this would be a greenfield project. SLUS-00726 is the NTSC-U release; main executable on disc will be SLUS_007.26.",
          "decomp.wiki (https://decomp.wiki/en/platforms/playstation, 'Decompedia') is JS-rendered and returned no body text to fetchers on two attempts; its mirror wiki.deco.mp refused connections (ECONNREFUSED). Facts above were instead sourced from splat/maspsx/old-gcc/decomp.me repos, project repos, and PCSX-Redux docs. A useful Ghidra-for-PSX walkthrough that is readable: https://tetracorp.github.io/tokimeki-memorial/methods/decompiling-psx-games.html."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Extract game files: dump the BFM disc image, pull SLUS_007.26 (and any overlay files) into disks/us/; record sha1 of the original EXE.",
          "Generate splat config: `python3 -m splat create_config disks/us/SLUS_007.26` — yields platform: psx, compiler: PSYQ, gp_value from header, header segment at 0x0, code segment at 0x800 with vram from EXE header.",
          "Set up repo skeleton per community convention (config/, src/, include/, asm/, tools/ with asm-differ/maspsx/m2c/decomp-permuter submodules, Makefile with extract/all/clean targets, diff_settings.py); nix-decomp-psx or esa/open-ribbon Makefiles are the references to copy.",
          "Identify the original compiler+assembler version empirically: compile a few recognizable functions (libc/libgs-adjacent or simple leaf functions) on decomp.me against gcc2.6.0-psx / gcc2.6.3-psx / gcc2.7.2-psx / psyq presets with -O2 -G0 variants until codegen patterns match; pick the matching --aspsx-version for maspsx the same way (nop placement and $gp usage are the tells).",
          "Install build toolchain in WSL2: prebuilt compiler from decompals/old-gcc releases (e.g. GCC 2.6.3 mipsel), apt binutils/gcc-mips-linux-gnu, pip `splat64[mips]`.",
          "`make extract` (splat split) to produce asm/ + the .ld linker script; build pipeline per object: cpp -> old gcc (-O2 -G0 ...) -> maspsx --aspsx-version=X.XX --run-assembler -> mipsel-linux-gnu-as -> ld -> objcopy to PS-EXE; `sha1sum` compare = OK/FAIL.",
          "Per-function matching loop: choose a function in asm/, draft C with m2c, place in src/ replacing the INCLUDE_ASM macro, rebuild, compare with asm-differ (`./tools/asm-differ/diff.py -mwo <func>`), iterate; use decomp-permuter and decomp.me scratches for stubborn diffs.",
          "Run static RE in Ghidra (ghidra_psx_ldr loads the PS-EXE and tags PsyQ functions) driven via Ghidra MCP for naming/typing; cross-check behavior live in PCSX-Redux (GDB server :3333 for Ghidra debugger, web API :8080 for RAM dumps, Lua console for memory watch/trace).",
          "Set up CI early: GitHub Actions ubuntu job that installs the toolchain, runs make extract + make, asserts checksum; never commit game binaries (use encrypted blob + secret like open-ribbon, or require local copy).",
          "Add progress tracking once functions start matching: request a frogress project slug + API key from ethteck, upload per-commit stats via frogress cli.py in CI; optionally pursue a decomp.dev listing later."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "splat64 — pip: `python3 -m pip install -U splat64[mips]` (pin `>=0.41.0,<1.0.0`); pulls spimdisasm/rabbitizer",
          "old-gcc prebuilt mipsel compilers (GCC 2.6.3 / 2.7.x / 2.8.1 candidates for BFM) — GitHub Releases at https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc (Linux binaries; run in WSL2)",
          "maspsx — git submodule https://github.com/mkst/maspsx; invoke `python3 maspsx.py --aspsx-version=X.XX --run-assembler`",
          "GNU binutils for mipsel (mipsel-linux-gnu-as/ld/objcopy) — apt `gcc-mips-linux-gnu`/`binutils-mipsel-linux-gnu`; beware >=2.38 regression, 2.35 known-good per open-ribbon",
          "asm-differ — https://github.com/simonlindholm/asm-differ (diff.py + diff_settings.py)",
          "m2c — https://github.com/matt-kempster/m2c (MIPS asm -> draft C)",
          "decomp-permuter — https://github.com/simonlindholm/decomp-permuter",
          "decomp.me — web scratchpad with gcc2.6.3-psx/gcc2.7.2-psx/psyq presets for compiler identification and sharing WIP matches",
          "PCSX-Redux — native Windows x64 nightly from https://distrib.app/pub/org/pcsx-redux/project/dev-win-x64; Lua scripting, MIPS debugger, GDB server :3333, web API :8080",
          "Ghidra (10.3+; 11.x current) + ghidra_psx_ldr extension — https://github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr (PS-EXE loader + PsyQ signature identification); gdb-multiarch for live debugging",
          "frogress client (cli.py from https://github.com/decompals/frogress) against https://progress.deco.mp",
          "Python 3.8+ and GNU make (WSL2 Ubuntu recommended for the whole build side)"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Runs natively on Windows: Ghidra + ghidra_psx_ldr, PCSX-Redux (first-class Windows x64 builds with Lua/GDB/web-API), decomp.me (web), and the MGS-style alternative pipeline (original PSYQ CC1PSX.EXE/ASPSX.EXE are Windows executables — mgs_reversing builds natively on Windows via `python3 build.py`, needing Wine only on Linux/macOS). Requires Linux/WSL2: the canonical modern matching pipeline — decompals/old-gcc prebuilt compilers are Linux mipsel-cross binaries, and binutils mipsel-linux-gnu plus the Makefile conventions (sotn/open-ribbon/esa) all assume a Linux environment; splat/maspsx/m2c/asm-differ/decomp-permuter are Python and could run on Windows but every reference project wires them together with Linux paths and apt toolchains. Practical split for this project: WSL2 Ubuntu for extract/build/diff loop; Windows host for Ghidra (MCP server) and PCSX-Redux; Ghidra-to-Redux GDB debugging works across the boundary via localhost:3333 (needs a gdb-multiarch binary — third-party download on Windows, apt on WSL2).",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "The 'gcc 2.6.3 era' assumption is unverified for Brave Fencer Musashi. BFM is a mid-1998 Square title; Square's FF7 (early 1997) used psyq <=4.1 (gcc 2.7.2), and 1998 titles commonly used psyq 4.x (gcc 2.7.2/2.8.1). gcc 2.6.3 is the SOTN/Konami-1997 profile. Identify empirically on decomp.me before committing to a toolchain, or early non-matches will be misdiagnosed as code problems.",
          "binutils-mipsel-linux-gnu >= 2.38 generated broken binaries (open-ribbon README, Ubuntu 22.04+); known-good fallback is 2.35. Pin or verify assembler version when the build mysteriously mismatches.",
          "maspsx forces -G0 to GNU as by default; if BFM was built with a non-zero $gp (-G4/-G8), you must pass -G8 to maspsx and reconsider --dont-force-G0 — wrong ASPSX version emulation changes nop scheduling and $gp-relative codegen (behavior shifts at 2.30/2.67/2.77/2.86).",
          "decomp.wiki is unreadable to non-JS fetchers (both attempts returned title only) and its wiki.deco.mp mirror refused connections — do not plan on scraping it programmatically; use the tool repos' READMEs/wikis as ground truth.",
          "frogress requires manual onboarding (admin-created slug + API key via ethteck on Discord/GitHub) — request early if progress badges are wanted; decomp.dev has no self-serve documented onboarding for PS1 projects.",
          "Never commit original game binaries; reference projects either require a user-supplied disc dump (sotn, mgs) or store AES-256-CBC-encrypted blobs decrypted in CI from secrets (open-ribbon).",
          "PCSX-Redux Lua editor autosaves to pcsx.lua and reloads it on startup — a crashing script can wedge the emulator across restarts (delete pcsx.lua to recover); long-running Lua must yield via coroutines (~15ms/frame budget) or the UI hangs.",
          "Ghidra GDB debugging quirks: PCSX-Redux must be launched with `-interpreter -debugger -gdb` (dynarec breaks debugging); Ghidra <10.3.3 needs the MIPS def changed mips:4000 -> mips:3000; mandatory gdb-multiarch with `-i mi2`.",
          "decomp.me PS1 GCC presets compile via plain cpp+gcc with a bundled -B compiler dir — local builds must replicate the maspsx step or objects won't byte-match what decomp.me shows as 'OK'.",
          "splat is published as `splat64` on PyPI (not 'splat') — installing the wrong package or omitting the [mips] extra breaks PSX disassembly.",
          "BFM likely uses runtime overlays (most 1998 Square games do); each overlay needs its own splat segment/config with correct vram, exactly the problem sotn-decomp solved — budget for overlay mapping work in Ghidra (ghidra_psx_ldr supports overlay handling) before mass function matching."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "Which exact compiler (gcc 2.6.3 vs 2.7.x vs 2.8.1 / PSY-Q 3.x vs 4.x) and ASPSX version Brave Fencer Musashi SLUS-00726 was built with — needs empirical testing on decomp.me with functions from the actual EXE.",
          "Whether BFM's main EXE is accompanied by overlay files on disc (count, load addresses) — determines splat config complexity; needs ISO inspection.",
          "Whether sotn-decomp's exact CFLAGS transfer — sotn-decomp's PSX compile flags were not visible in its root Makefile (build logic moved; CMakeLists.txt now present at root), so the sibling sotn research should pin down its current cc invocation (likely in sotn.sh / tools or CMake).",
          "How decomp.dev ingests new PS1 projects (objdiff report format requirement?) — undocumented on the site; would need to read https://github.com/encounter/decomp.dev source or ask in the decomp Discord.",
          "Whether splat's current version auto-detects BFM's data/rodata boundaries well (find_file_boundaries) or whether heavy manual subsegment work is needed — only answerable by running create_config + split on the real EXE."
        ],
