    "gaps": [
      {
        "gap": "No PsyQ-SDK-release -> GCC(CC1PSX) version -> ASPSX version mapping table is in the findings. The digest documents HOW to detect BFM's PsyQ version (ghidra_psx_ldr DetectPsyQ 'Ps' stamp) and lists maspsx --aspsx-version behavior tiers (2.21/2.34/2.56...), but nothing translates a detected SDK version (e.g. '4.1') into the concrete toolchain triple needed for the splat/Makefile config: which cc1 build from decompals/old-gcc + which --aspsx-version flag + default flags. Also unverified: which toolchains other late-1998 Square PS1 titles used (only FF7 = psyq 4.1/gcc 2.7.2 is captured), and whether decomp.me already has any BFM scratch.",
        "why_it_matters": "The entire matching-decomp phase is blocked until the compiler is pinned. Without the SDK->GCC->ASPSX table, even a successful DetectPsyQ result at Ghidra import cannot be turned into a build pipeline; the context document would have to hand-wave the single most load-bearing configuration decision, and a wrong guess (e.g. copying sotn's gcc 2.6.3-psx for a Sep-1998 build) would silently produce near-miss diffs for every function.",
        "followup_prompt": "Build the authoritative mapping of PlayStation PsyQ SDK releases to bundled compiler/assembler versions, for configuring a matching decomp of Brave Fencer Musashi (SLUS-00726, US master built Sep 22 1998, Square). 1) Produce a table: PsyQ SDK version (3.5, 3.6, 4.0, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4, 4.6) -> release date -> bundled CC1PSX/GCC version -> bundled ASPSX.EXE version -> corresponding decompals/old-gcc release artifact -> correct maspsx --aspsx-version flag. Sources to fetch: https://github.com/mkst/maspsx README and issues (ASPSX version notes), https://psx.arthus.net/sdk/ directory listing (SDK archives with dates/contents), decomp.me backend https://github.com/decompals/decomp.me blob coreapp/compilers.py and any presets named psyq3.5/psyq3.6/psyq4.0/gcc2.7.2-psx, and https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc releases. 2) Search for the compilers used by other 1997-1998 Square PS1 decomp/RE efforts (Xenogears, Parasite Eve, Einhänder, FF7, FF8 demo, Chocobo's Mysterious Dungeon) — check decomp.me preset list (https://decomp.me/api/preset?platform=ps1 or the presets page), GitHub repos, and the splat wiki Examples page — to establish what Square's US localization builds of that exact window shipped with. 3) Check decomp.me for any existing Brave Fencer Musashi scratch (search the site and Google site:decomp.me musashi). Report exact version numbers, URLs, and the recommended candidate ordering for fingerprinting BFM (e.g. try psyq 4.1/gcc 2.7.2 + aspsx 2.56 first)."
      },
      {
        "gap": "The reference implementation of BFM's .CD container + LZSS format — CUE's 'Brave Fencer extractor' (BRAVE v1.0, romhacking.net utility #939, C source included) — was located but never actually retrieved or read. Findings reconstructed the TOC layout and LZSS state machine independently, but the tool's source would confirm: how compressed vs raw sub-files are distinguished, whether SC01-07.CD use the same TOC as MAIN.CD, what LIST.CD's second sector holds, and any sub-file type knowledge (code overlays vs assets). The romhacking forum thread topic=26396.0 ('[PSX] Brave Fencer Musashi') is similarly unread.",
        "why_it_matters": "Disc extraction and overlay mapping are the two roadmap phases immediately after Ghidra setup; the AP-world code patches at 0x8013xxxx-0x8018xxxx prove substantial code loads above the 413KB EXE (i.e. overlays inside the .CD archives), so the extraction tool's format knowledge directly determines the splat segment layout. Re-deriving format details that a published, source-available tool already encodes wastes effort and risks subtle errors (e.g. mis-detecting which sub-files are compressed would corrupt every extracted overlay).",
        "followup_prompt": "Retrieve and analyze the source code of 'Brave Fencer extractor' (BRAVE v1.0 by CUE, 2013-03-16), romhacking.net utility #939, for the .CD container and LZSS formats of Brave Fencer Musashi (PS1). The live site Cloudflare-blocks scripts: use the Wayback Machine — try http://web.archive.org/web/2023/https://www.romhacking.net/utilities/939/ for the page and http://web.archive.org/web/2023*/https://www.romhacking.net/download/utilities/939/* (or the dl.romhacking.net asset URL embedded in the snapshot) for the ZIP; also try archive.org full-text search for 'BRAVE CUE Musashi', CUE's posts on romhacking/zenhax/xentax mirrors (xentax wiki mirrors at wiki.vg-resource or the zenhax archive), and GitHub mirrors of romhacking utilities (e.g. github.com/romhack, search code for 'MUSASHI' lzss). From the C source report verbatim: TOC parsing logic (entry struct, sector size assumptions), how it decides a sub-file is LZSS-compressed vs stored (magic? size heuristic? per-entry flag?), whether it processes MAIN.CD, SC01-SC07.CD and LIST.CD identically, the exact LZSS parameters (ring size, index bits, length bias, terminator), and whether it can REPACK (or extract only). Also fetch a readable mirror of romhacking.net forum thread index.php?topic=26396.0 ('[PSX] Brave Fencer Musashi') via Wayback and summarize any format/overlay/text-table findings in it."
      },
      {
        "gap": "Unresolved contradiction about which Ghidra MCP server to standardize on. The findings pin stock LaurieWired GhidraMCP 1.4 (27 tools, Ghidra 11.3.2 max, effectively unmaintained, no struct/data-type creation tools) — yet the psxrecomp project being held up as the proof-of-concept used MCP tool names (mcp__ghidra__get_function_info, xrefs, list_data) and an SSE server on localhost:7777 that do NOT match LaurieWired's bridge, meaning the one validated Claude-Code-on-PS1 workflow ran on a different, unidentified server. Alternative servers (bethington/ghidra-mcp '200+ tools, Ghidra 12.0.3, headless', ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12, pyghidra-mcp) are name-dropped but unaudited.",
        "why_it_matters": "The project plan routes 'nearly all reverse engineering' through GhidraMCP, and a matching decomp needs heavy data-type work (struct creation, applying PsyQ .gdt types, retyping globals) that LaurieWired 1.4's 27 tools largely lack. Writing the context doc around the wrong server bakes in either a dead-end (unmaintained 11.3.2-only stock build vs ghidra_psx_ldr now targeting 12.x) or an unvetted fork; it also misrepresents what psxrecomp actually proved.",
        "followup_prompt": "Determine which Ghidra MCP server to use for a Claude Code-driven PS1 decompilation on Windows, resolving what psxrecomp actually ran. 1) Inspect https://github.com/mstan/psxrecomp: fetch .mcp.json, the tools/ directory listing, docs (CLAUDE.md, PLAN.md), and search the repo (GitHub code search 'in:file 7777 repo:mstan/psxrecomp', 'get_function_info') to identify the MCP server behind http://localhost:7777/sse with tools named get_function_info/xrefs/list_data — is it LaurieWired GhidraMCP, a fork, or a custom server bundled in the repo? Check the author's blog/articles (search 'mstan psxrecomp ghidra mcp') for the exact plugin named. 2) Survey currently maintained Ghidra MCP servers compatible with Ghidra 12.x: bethington/ghidra-mcp (verify claimed Ghidra 12.0.3 support, tool count, struct/data-type tools: create_struct, apply_data_type, set_global_data_type, headless/pyghidra mode, last commit date), ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12, clearbluejar's pyghidra-mcp (PyPI page, headless multi-program support), GhidrAssistMCP (jtang613), and LaurieWired GhidraMCP issues #83/#131 for any merged 12.x fix. For each: latest release/commit date, supported Ghidra version, transport (stdio/sse/http), and presence of data-type/struct editing + symbol rename + decompile tools. 3) Conclude with a concrete recommendation (server + Ghidra version + ghidra_psx_ldr release zip pairing) for Ghidra 12.1 if achievable, else the best 11.3.2 fallback, with install commands."
      },
      {
        "gap": "No concrete facts captured about the Windows 11 <-> WSL2 split-brain workflow the roadmap mandates (Windows-native Ghidra + Claude Code at Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp, Ubuntu 24.04 build tree in WSL2). Missing: the documented ~5-20x filesystem performance penalty of building on /mnt/<drive> (9P/NTFS) vs WSL ext4 and the resulting repo-placement decision; whether one repo can serve both sides (\\\\wsl.localhost\\ access, git core.autocrlf/permissions pitfalls, file-watcher inotify non-propagation across 9P); how to invoke WSL builds from Windows-side Claude Code (wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd ... syntax/quoting); and WSL2 networking direction-of-reachability (can a WSL process reach the Windows-host GhidraMCP HTTP server on 127.0.0.1:8080? — under default NAT it cannot via localhost; needs host IP from /etc/resolv.conf or networkingMode=mirrored in .wslconfig).",
        "why_it_matters": "Every daily-loop command in the context doc (make extract/build in WSL, asm-differ watch mode, Ghidra MCP calls, decompile.py) crosses this boundary. Getting repo placement wrong makes every build 10x slower and breaks watch tooling; getting networking wrong means any WSL-side script that wants to query Ghidra silently can't connect. These are well-documented Microsoft/WSL facts that the doc currently asserts nothing about, so Claude Code would rediscover them by trial and error.",
        "followup_prompt": "Compile verified Windows 11 + WSL2 interop facts for a project where Ghidra (with an MCP HTTP server on 127.0.0.1:8080) and Claude Code run on native Windows while a PS1 decomp build tree (make/splat/gcc/asm-differ watch mode) runs in WSL2 Ubuntu 24.04. From Microsoft Learn (learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/filesystems, /wsl/networking, /wsl/wsl-config) and corroborating sources, report: 1) Filesystem: documented performance guidance for cross-OS file access (/mnt/c 9P penalty vs ext4), whether inotify/file-watchers work on /mnt paths and whether Windows file-watchers work on \\\\wsl.localhost\\ paths, git pitfalls of sharing one working tree across both sides (core.autocrlf, file mode bits, index refresh), and the recommended placement for a repo that both Windows Ghidra and WSL builds must touch (including whether Ghidra can open project files over \\\\wsl.localhost\\ reliably). 2) Command invocation: exact syntax for running builds in WSL from Windows (wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd /home/user/bfm -- bash -lc 'make -j build'), exit-code propagation, and UNC-path cwd limitations. 3) Networking: under default NAT mode, can WSL2 processes reach a Windows-host service bound to 127.0.0.1:8080 (answer with the documented mechanism: Windows localhost->WSL works via localhostForwarding, WSL->Windows requires the host IP from `ip route`/resolv.conf or firewall-permitted vEthernet address), and what networkingMode=mirrored in %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig changes (requirements: Windows 11 22H2+, behavior: shared localhost both directions), plus any GhidraMCP-specific need to bind 0.0.0.0 instead of 127.0.0.1. Cite each fact's URL and give a recommended layout (repo in WSL ext4 vs Windows NTFS) with rationale."
      }
