    "followups": [
      {
        "topic": "PsyQ SDK -> GCC(CC1PSX) -> ASPSX version mapping for pinning the Brave Fencer Musashi (SLUS-00726, US master 1998-09-22) matching-decomp toolchain",
        "key_facts": [
          "AUTHORITATIVE SDK->TOOLCHAIN TABLE (extracted directly from the binaries in mkst/esa 'psyq-binaries' release tarballs, which decomp.me itself uses; version strings quoted from CC1PSX.EXE/ASPSX.EXE): PsyQ 3.3 (DTL-S2190) = GNU C 2.6.0 [AL 1.1, MM 40] + ASPSX 2.21 (ASPSX.EXE dated 1995-06-22); PsyQ 3.5 (DTL-S2300) = GNU C 2.6.0 (same 1994-10-07 CC1PSX binary) + ASPSX 2.34 (1996-06-16); PsyQ 3.6 (DTL-S2310) = GNU C/C++ 2.7.2.SN.1 [AL 1.1, MM 40] + ASPSX 2.34 (1996-02-19); PsyQ 4.0 (DTL-S2320) = GNU C 2.7.2.SN32.3.7.0002 + ASPSX 2.56; PsyQ 4.1 (DTL-S2330) = 'GNU C version cygnus-2.7.2-970404 SN32.3.7.0004 (SonyPSX)' + ASPSX 2.67 (both binaries dated 1997-09-21); PsyQ 4.2 = library-update release only, NO toolchain/RTL disc exists in the redump set (archive.org ps1_sdks has 3.0/3.3/3.5/3.6/4.0/4.1/4.3/4.4/4.6/4.7 but no 4.2); PsyQ 4.3 (DTL-S2340) = 'GNU C version 2.8.0 SN32 Build 4.0.0007 (SonyPlayStation)' + ASPSX 2.77; PsyQ 4.4 (DTL-S2350) = 'GNU C version 2.8.1 SN32 BUILD 4.0.0010 (PSX)' + ASPSX 2.79 (binaries dated 1998-10-05); PsyQ 4.5 (SDevTC MFD) = 'GNU C version egcs-2.91.66 19990314 (egcs-1.1.2 release) (PSX)' + ASPSX 2.81; PsyQ 4.6 (DTL-S2360) = 'GNU C version 2.95.2 19991024 BUILD 4.0.0030 (PSX)' + ASPSX 2.86.",
          "Special combo: decomp.me compiler id 'psyq_263_221' = CC1PSX GCC 2.6.3 + ASPSX 2.21 (verified by downloading https://github.com/decompme/compilers/releases/download/compilers/psyq_263_221.tar.gz) — this is the SOTN-era mix from the separate 'GNU C Compiler 2.6.3' floppy; it is the WRONG era for BFM, do not copy sotn-decomp's compiler setting.",
          "DIRECT FINGERPRINT OF BFM ITSELF: replicated ghidra_psx_ldr DetectPsyQ logic (pattern 'Ps' [0x50 0x73], mask FF FF E0 00 00 00 88 EE, version u16 big-endian at offset +6) in Python against the local 'Brave Fencer Musashi (USA) (Track 1).bin'. Result: PS-X EXE header at track offset 0xdc98; within the EXE region, 13 library stamps decode to version 0x4000 -> '400' = PsyQ 4.0 (libnums 2,3,4,6,7,8,9,14,17,24), one stamp 0x4200 -> '420' = PsyQ 4.2 (libnum 0), one 0x4010 = 4.0.1x (libnum 16), one 0x4210 = 4.2.1x (libnum 12). Conclusion: BFM links PsyQ 4.0 libraries with a couple of 4.2 library updates -> the toolchain is the GCC 2.7.2 SN32 (cygnus-2.7.2-970404) generation, NOT 2.8.x. Ghidra's DetectPsyQ will most likely report '4.0' (first stamp in memory order); load data/psyq400.gdt (psyq420.gdt also exists).",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr ships type archives psyq260/300/330/340/350/3610/3611/370/400/410/420/430/440/450/460/470.gdt plus per-version function signature JSONs under data/psyq/; old versions (260-350) are detected via LIBGPU.LIB/SYS.OBJ signature fallback (source: src/main/java/psyq/DetectPsyQ.java).",
          "maspsx --aspsx-version thresholds (exact, from maspsx.py config_for_aspsx_version): div_uses_tge if 1.10<v<2.10; nop_at_expansion+addiu_at+no-mflo/mfhi-nop if v<2.30; expand_li OFF if v>=2.50; sltu_at OFF if v>=2.60; gp_allow_offset ON if v>=2.70; gp_allow_la ON if v>=2.80. CRITICAL: omitting --aspsx-version gives the dataclass defaults (nop_mflo_mfhi=True, sltu_at=True, expand_li=True, no gp offset) which behaves like ASPSX ~2.3x, NOT the newest — always pass the flag explicitly. So: psyq4.0 -> --aspsx-version=2.56, psyq4.1 -> --aspsx-version=2.67, psyq4.3 -> 2.77, psyq4.4 -> 2.79, psyq4.5 -> 2.81, psyq4.6 -> 2.86.",
          "decompals/old-gcc release 0.17 (latest, all built for Linux x86_64, some macOS) artifacts: gcc-2.5.7-psx, gcc-2.6.0-psx, gcc-2.6.3-psx, gcc-2.7.0, gcc-2.7.1, gcc-2.7.2, gcc-2.7.2-psx, gcc-2.7.2-cdk (= cygnus-2.7.2-970404, the actual base of PsyQ 4.0/4.1 CC1PSX; added in old-gcc 0.14), gcc-2.7.2.1/2/3, gcc-2.8.0-psx, gcc-2.8.1-psx, gcc-2.91.66-psx, gcc-2.95.2-psx (+-macos variants). Download pattern: https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc/releases/download/0.17/gcc-2.7.2-psx.tar.gz",
          "decomp.me PS1 compiler ids (backend/coreapp/compilers.py in decompme/decomp.me — NOTE repo is decompme/, not decompals/): psyq_263_221, psyq3.3, psyq3.5, psyq3.6 (run CC1PSX.EXE under dosemu), psyq4.0, psyq4.3 (run CC1PSX.EXE+ASPSX.EXE under wibo, then psyq-obj-parser to ELF), psyq4.1, psyq4.4, psyq4.5, psyq4.6 (run CCPSX.EXE driver under wibo with generated SN.INI containing compiler_path/assembler_path/tmpdir), and maspsx-based ids gcc2.5.7-psx, gcc2.6.0-psx, gcc2.6.3-psx, gcc2.7.2-cdk, gcc2.7.2-psx, gcc2.8.0-psx, gcc2.8.1-psx, gcc2.91.66-psx, gcc2.95.2-psx, plus vanilla gcc2.7.0/2.7.1/2.7.2.1/2.7.2.2/2.7.2.3-mipsel. The gcc*-psx images bundle old-gcc cc1 + maspsx pinned at commit 874855c53f65f8fa57447e1da6bde6236dbef9d5 with wrapper 'as' = python3 maspsx.py --run-assembler.",
          "Original PsyQ tool binaries per SDK are downloadable individually: https://github.com/mkst/esa/releases/download/psyq-binaries/psyq{3.3,3.5,3.6,4.0,4.1,4.3,4.4,4.5,4.6}.tar.gz (1-2.3MB each; contain CC1PSX.EXE, ASPSX.EXE, CCPSX.EXE, PSYLINK.EXE, PSYLIB.EXE etc. — Win32 PE, runnable natively on Windows or via wibo/wine on Linux). Full SDK installs: https://psx.arthus.net/sdk/Psy-Q/PSYQ_SDK.zip (140MB) and archive.org item 'ps1_sdks' (redump RTL discs: 3.3=DTL-S2190, 3.5=DTL-S2300, 3.6=DTL-S2310, 4.0=DTL-S2320, 4.1=DTL-S2330, 4.3=DTL-S2340, 4.4=DTL-S2350, 4.6=DTL-S2360).",
          "SQUARE 1997-98 PRECEDENT #1 — Xenogears US (SLUS-00664, released Oct 1998, master ~Aug 1998, the closest comparable to BFM): matching decomp at https://github.com/ladysilverberg/xenogears-decomp. Its gears.toml build presets: Default (game code) = gcc-2.7.2-psx cc1, cc_flags '-mips1 -mcpu=3000 -w -funsigned-char -fpeephole -ffunction-cse -fpcc-struct-return -fcommon -fverbose-asm -msoft-float -mgas -fgnu-linker -quiet', -O2, -G8, maspsx '--use-comm-section --run-assembler' (no aspsx-version flag); 'PSY-Q' preset (SDK library code) = '--aspsx-version=2.56 --expand-div', -O3, -G0; some modules use gcc-2.6.0-psx and gcc-2.7.2-cdk-psx with --aspsx-version=2.67. I.e. one Square EXE mixes three cc1 builds.",
          "SQUARE PRECEDENT #2 — FF7 US (SCUS-94163, 1997): https://github.com/Drahsid/ffvii Makefile uses native cc1 2.7.2 as default CC with CC_FLAGS '-G 0 -mips1 -mcpu=3000 -mgas -msoft-float -O2 -fgnu-linker'; comments map tools/psyq/3.6=2.7.2.SN.1, 4.1=cygnus-2.7.2-970404, 4.3='2.8.1 SN32' (imprecise; actual 4.3 binary is 2.8.0 Build 4.0.0007), 4.6=2.95. splat wiki Examples lists FF7 as 'psyq <=4.1 (gcc2.7.2)'. Other splat examples: Soul Reaver = psyq4.3, ESA = psyq4.6, Silent Hill = 'psyq <=4.1 (gcc2.7.2) TBD'. Xenogears non-matching reimplementation also exists (yaz0r/Noah); Parasite Eve 2 (not PE1) decomp at GabeRealB/parasite-eve-2-decomp. No Einhänder/Chocobo/SaGa Frontier matching decomps found.",
          "RECOMMENDED CANDIDATE ORDER FOR BFM FINGERPRINTING: (1) gcc2.7.2-psx (old-gcc) + maspsx --aspsx-version=2.56, flags '-O2 -G0 -mips1 -mcpu=3000 -mgas -msoft-float -fgnu-linker' (FF7 style; check BFM's gp usage — if $gp-relative data appears, try -G8 like Xenogears); (2) same cc1 + --aspsx-version=2.67 (PsyQ 4.1 assembler era); (3) gcc2.7.2-cdk (cygnus-2.7.2-970404, exact CC1PSX 4.0/4.1 base) with 2.56 then 2.67; (4) the real PsyQ 4.0/4.1 CC1PSX.EXE+ASPSX.EXE from mkst/esa tarballs run natively on Windows + psyq-obj-parser, for byte-exact arbitration; (5) only if diffs show GCC 2.8-style codegen, fall back to gcc2.8.0-psx + 2.77 (psyq4.3) — unlikely since the JP master predates Jul 16 1998. For SDK library code inside the EXE use --aspsx-version=2.56 --expand-div -O3 -G0 (Xenogears precedent, matches the 4.0 lib stamps).",
          "decomp.me preset/scratch search for Brave Fencer Musashi: decomp.me API (https://decomp.me/api/preset?platform=ps1, /api/scratch?search=musashi) is behind a Cloudflare managed challenge — returns 403/JS challenge to curl and WebFetch. Google web search finds no BFM scratch, no BFM decomp repo on GitHub; only romhacking tools (a 'Brave Fencer extractor' on romhacking.net, LZSS-variant text compression notes at github.com/pleonex/AiroRom/wiki/Brave-fencer-Musashi). The field is empty — this would be the first BFM decomp project.",
          "psyq-obj-parser (converts PsyQ .OBJ to ELF, needed when using real ASPSX.EXE instead of maspsx): part of pcsx-redux (github.com/grumpycoders/pcsx-redux/tree/main/tools/psyq-obj-parser); prebuilt Linux binary at https://github.com/decompme/compilers/releases/download/compilers/psyq-obj-parser.tar.gz",
          "Decompedia wiki moved: old wiki.deco.mp is dead (DNS NXDOMAIN); new https://decomp.wiki is a JS-rendered SPA unreadable via WebFetch; markdown source lives at github.com/decompals/decompedia (platforms/playstation.md confirms PSYQ=GCC-derived, recommends maspsx/psyq-obj-parser/ghidra_psx_ldr, but contains no version table)."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Confirm PsyQ version at Ghidra import: ghidra_psx_ldr DetectPsyQ on SLUS_007.26 should report 4.0 (pre-verified here by scanning Track 1: 13x 'Ps' stamps = 4.0, plus 4.2 stamps on libnums 0 and 12); load psyq400.gdt types and apply 4.0 library signatures so SDK functions are excluded from decomp targets.",
          "Extract SLUS_007.26 and overlays from the disc image (e.g. with mkpsxiso/dumpsxiso or psximager under WSL2) and set up splat with platform: psx, compiler: PSYQ; read gp_value from the EXE header (0x80xxxxxx) to decide -G0 vs -G8.",
          "Download toolchain: https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc/releases/download/0.17/gcc-2.7.2-psx.tar.gz (and gcc-2.7.2-cdk.tar.gz as alternate) for WSL2; git clone https://github.com/mkst/maspsx; apt install binutils-mips-linux-gnu (mips-linux-gnu-as for maspsx --run-assembler); optionally fetch real binaries https://github.com/mkst/esa/releases/download/psyq-binaries/psyq4.0.tar.gz and psyq4.1.tar.gz for native-Windows arbitration.",
          "Fingerprint the compiler on 3-5 medium-sized leaf functions: compile with cc1 (gcc2.7.2-psx) flags '-quiet -O2 -G0 -mips1 -mcpu=3000 -mgas -msoft-float -fgnu-linker' piped to 'maspsx.py --aspsx-version=2.56 --run-assembler', diff with asm-differ/objdiff; permute aspsx-version 2.56 vs 2.67, -G0 vs -G8, -O2 vs -O3, -funsigned-char on/off until regalloc and div/li/gp idioms match.",
          "Lock the winning triple into the Makefile (Xenogears' gears.toml and FF7's Makefile are the templates); use per-directory presets: game code preset vs SDK-library preset (--aspsx-version=2.56 --expand-div -O3 -G0).",
          "Verify full-binary match with sha1 against the original EXE (splat check pattern: sha256sum --check config/checksum.sha as in xenogears-decomp)."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "decompals/old-gcc 0.17 cc1 builds: gcc-2.7.2-psx.tar.gz (primary candidate) and gcc-2.7.2-cdk.tar.gz (cygnus-2.7.2-970404, exact PsyQ 4.0/4.1 CC1PSX base) — Linux x86_64 binaries, run in WSL2",
          "mkst/maspsx (python3, no pinned version needed; decomp.me pins commit 874855c53f65f8fa57447e1da6bde6236dbef9d5) + binutils mipsel/mips-linux-gnu 'as' (apt: binutils-mips-linux-gnu); invoke: python3 maspsx.py --aspsx-version=2.56 --run-assembler",
          "Original PsyQ Win32 binaries per SDK: https://github.com/mkst/esa/releases/download/psyq-binaries/psyq4.0.tar.gz, psyq4.1.tar.gz (CC1PSX.EXE/ASPSX.EXE/CCPSX.EXE/PSYLINK.EXE) — native on Windows, or via wibo/wine on Linux",
          "psyq-obj-parser (pcsx-redux) — PsyQ .OBJ -> ELF when using real ASPSX; prebuilt: https://github.com/decompme/compilers/releases/download/compilers/psyq-obj-parser.tar.gz",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr (lab313ru) with psyq400.gdt/psyq420.gdt and data/psyq signature JSONs",
          "splat (ethteck/splat, platform: psx) + spimdisasm; asm-differ/objdiff for diffing; m2c for decompilation drafts",
          "WSL2 Ubuntu (for dosemu/wibo/maspsx/binutils pipeline); wibo (decomp.me's lightweight Win32 loader) optional on Linux",
          "Python 3.14 already at C:\\Users\\user\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Python\\Python314\\python (used here for the stamp scan; script left at /tmp/detect_psyq2.py in the Git Bash filesystem)"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "CC1PSX.EXE/ASPSX.EXE/CCPSX.EXE/PSYLINK.EXE are Win32 binaries that run NATIVELY on Windows 11 (psyq 4.x ones; 3.x are DOS-era, decomp.me runs those under dosemu) — useful for byte-exact arbitration builds. The standard community pipeline (old-gcc cc1 Linux binaries + maspsx + mipsel-linux-gnu-as + splat Makefiles + psyq-obj-parser prebuilts) is Linux x86_64-first: run it in WSL2. decomp.me runs the Win32 tools under wibo on Linux. Ghidra+ghidra_psx_ldr and Python tooling (splat, m2c, asm-differ) run natively on Windows; the disc-stamp scan was done natively with Windows Python.",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "Do NOT copy sotn-decomp's compiler (psyq_263_221 = GCC 2.6.3 + ASPSX 2.21): BFM's library stamps prove a PsyQ 4.0/4.2 link, i.e. GCC 2.7.2 SN32 era — a 2.6.3 cc1 would produce systematic near-miss diffs.",
          "maspsx with NO --aspsx-version flag is not 'latest behavior': the defaults equal roughly ASPSX 2.3x-2.4x (expand_li=True, sltu_at=True). Xenogears exploits this for game code, but for BFM always set the flag explicitly per experiment to keep results interpretable.",
          "'Ps' stamps date the LINKED LIBRARIES, not the compiler that built game code; Square mixed toolchains within one EXE (Xenogears uses gcc-2.7.2-psx, gcc-2.6.0-psx, and gcc-2.7.2-cdk-psx in the same binary). Expect per-module variation in BFM; fingerprint several modules independently.",
          "PsyQ 4.3's CC1PSX in the mkst/esa package is 'GNU C 2.8.0 SN32 Build 4.0.0007' + ASPSX 2.77, although community shorthand (splat wiki, FF7 Makefile comment) often says '4.3 = gcc 2.8.1' — 2.8.1 BUILD 4.0.0010 is actually the 4.4 compiler. If you ever need 2.8-era, verify which one you mean.",
          "PsyQ '4.2' has no recoverable toolchain disc (absent from redump/arthus sets); if DetectPsyQ reports 4.2 stamps, still use the 4.1 toolchain (ASPSX 2.67) or 4.0 (2.56) — 4.2 was a library refresh.",
          "DetectPsyQ reads the version short BIG-endian (mem.getShort(addr, true)) despite PSX being LE — when replicating the scan, decoding LE gives wrong versions ('40' instead of '400').",
          "Raw .bin track scans include 2352-byte sectors (sync/ECC); 8-byte stamps can straddle 2048-byte user-data boundaries and false positives occur in compressed assets — prefer scanning the extracted SLUS_007.26 for the final word.",
          "decomp.me API is Cloudflare-challenged (403 to curl/WebFetch) — scratch/preset checks must be done manually in a browser; GitHub unauthenticated API rate limit (60/hr) was hit during research, use raw.githubusercontent.com or authenticated gh instead.",
          "Old reference sites are decaying: wiki.deco.mp is dead (use github.com/decompals/decompedia markdown), psxdev.net returns 403 to fetchers, web.archive.org unreachable from this environment."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "Which exact ASPSX tier does BFM game code match: 2.56 (PsyQ 4.0) or 2.67 (PsyQ 4.1)? The 2.56->2.67 observable difference is 'use $at for sltu < 0' (present at 2.56, gone at 2.67) and %hi/%lo macro support; decide empirically on functions containing unsigned < 0 comparisons or li/sltu idioms.",
          "Does BFM use $gp-relative addressing (-G8) like Xenogears or -G0 like FF7? Read gp_value from the SLUS header and check for $gp loads in Ghidra before fixing the flag.",
          "What ASPSX version shipped with the PsyQ 4.2 library update (if any separate assembler existed)? No 4.2 toolchain archive is known to survive.",
          "Are there existing BFM scratches on decomp.me? Programmatic search blocked by Cloudflare; needs one manual browser check at https://decomp.me/search (Google shows none).",
          "Exact release dates of PsyQ 4.0/4.1/4.2/4.3 RTL discs (binary dates recovered: 4.1 toolchain built 1997-09-21, 4.4 toolchain 1998-10-05; 4.0/4.3/4.5 tarballs were repacked and lost mtimes) — only matters for historiography, not for the build.",
          "Which libnum corresponds to which PsyQ library (libnum 0 = LIBSN? 2 = LIBAPI?) — mapping the 16 stamped libnums in BFM to library names would tell exactly which libs are 4.2-updated; ghidra_psx_ldr's signature pass will resolve this anyway."
        ],
        "sources": [
          "https://github.com/mkst/maspsx (README incl. ASPSX behavior table)",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mkst/maspsx/master/maspsx.py (config_for_aspsx_version thresholds)",
          "https://github.com/mkst/esa/releases (psyq-binaries tag; downloaded psyq3.3/3.5/3.6/4.0/4.1/4.3/4.4/4.5/4.6.tar.gz and extracted version strings + file dates)",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/decompme/decomp.me/main/backend/coreapp/compilers.py (PS1 compiler ids and cc commands)",
          "https://github.com/decompme/compilers (platforms/ps1 Dockerfiles; psyq_263_221.tar.gz downloaded and inspected)",
          "https://github.com/decompals/old-gcc/releases (0.17 asset list via API; release notes 0.9-0.17)",
          "https://psx.arthus.net/sdk/Psy-Q/ (directory listing)",
          "https://archive.org/download/ps1_sdks/ (redump RTL disc list with DTL-S numbers)",
          "https://github.com/ethteck/splat/wiki/Examples (per-game compiler list: FF7 psyq<=4.1, Soul Reaver psyq4.3, ESA psyq4.6)",
          "https://github.com/ladysilverberg/xenogears-decomp (cloned; gears.toml presets, config/slus_006.64.yaml, Makefile, Dockerfile, wiki)",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Drahsid/ffvii/master/Makefile (FF7 toolchain + flags)",
          "https://github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr (master tarball; src/main/java/psyq/DetectPsyQ.java, src/main/java/psx/PsxLoader.java, data/*.gdt list)",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/decompals/decompedia/main/platforms/playstation.md (Decompedia PS1 page source)",
          "https://www.retroreversing.com/Official-PlayStation-1-Software-Development-Kit (PSY-Q history; no version table)",
          "https://gamingdoc.org/software/platforms/consoles/sony-playstation/windows/official/sdk/psy-q/ (only 4.6/4.7 hosted)",
          "Local primary source: Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp\\Brave Fencer Musashi (USA)\\Brave Fencer Musashi (USA) (Track 1).bin (PsyQ 'Ps' stamp scan, script /tmp/detect_psyq2.py)",
          "Dead/blocked during research: https://wiki.deco.mp/index.php/PSY-Q (DNS dead, moved to decomp.wiki SPA), https://decomp.me/api/preset?platform=ps1 and /api/scratch?search=musashi (Cloudflare 403), https://www.psxdev.net/forum/viewtopic.php?t=4113 (403), web.archive.org (unreachable)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "CUE's BRAVE v1.0 extractor source (romhacking.net utility #939) — Brave Fencer Musashi .CD container + LZSS format, plus forum threads 26396/15730 and the jywjyw repacker repo",
        "key_facts": [
          "RETRIEVED THE ACTUAL TOOL: romhacking.net utility #939 download is a RAR4 archive (16,366 bytes) archived at http://web.archive.org/web/20131011153707if_/http://www.romhacking.net/download/utilities/939/ (identical digest in a 2017-07-31 capture, so v1.0 was never updated). Contents: brave.c (6,092 B, 2013-03-16), common.inc (8,221 B, CUE's helper lib: FileSize/FileLoad/FileSave/Allocate + KILO_/MEGA_ constants), brave.exe (7,680 B Win32 console), brave.txt (Spanish format doc), readme.txt, license.txt (GPLv3). Usage: 'BRAVE file [file [...]]' where file is any valid .CD from the game. EXTRACT/DECOMPRESS ONLY — no repack capability whatsoever.",
          ".CD TOC FORMAT (verbatim from brave.c Extract()): #define SECTOR_SIZE 0x800. Reads u32 total (file count), skips one u32 (always zero), then per entry two u32 LE: pos = entry_sector * 0x800 (sector index RELATIVE TO THE START OF THE .CD FILE, not a disc LBA — code does 'buffer + pos' on the loaded file) and len = byte length (unpadded). Header and every sub-file are sector-aligned, zero-padded. Sub-files extracted as <file>.dir/FILE_%03d. First data file starts at sector 1 (0x800).",
          "MAIN.CD AND SC01-SC07.CD ARE PROCESSED IDENTICALLY — one code path, no per-file special-casing. Confirmed by jywjyw's Conf.java: CDS = {MAIN, SC01..SC07} all rebuilt by the same CdRebuilder. Sub-file counts: MAIN.CD = 0x31 (49) sub-files (JP), SC01.CD = 0x56 (86), SC02.CD = 45 (per Auryn, US version). JP byte sizes (Conf.CD_JAPAN_SIZE): MAIN 6,205,440; SC01 20,615,168; SC02 6,580,224; SC03 78,168,064; SC04 20,764,672; SC05 16,502,784; SC06 23,908,352; SC07 12,730,368.",
          "LIST.CD: per brave.txt 'contiene las cabeceras de todos los archivos CD' (contains the headers of all the CD archives). jywjyw's ListCdWriter.java + CdHeader.trimBuild() show exactly how: LIST.CD = the 8 .CD TOCs (order MAIN, SC01..SC07) each TRIMMED to 8 + 8*N bytes (u32 count + u32 zero, then 8-byte entries) and concatenated back-to-back with NO sector alignment between tables, written into a fixed 0x1000-byte (2-sector) buffer. So LIST.CD's 'second sector' is just the continuation of the concatenated tables (the later SCxx TOCs). note.md: it is loaded into RAM at game start so the game can locate sub-files without reading each .CD's header sector. CUE's tool does NOT special-case LIST.CD (running BRAVE on it would misparse it).",
          "COMPRESSED-VS-RAW DETECTION: purely a per-entry TYPE FLAG, no magic and no size heuristic. Sub-files are 'PAC chains': each entry has a full 0x800-byte header: +0x00 u32 'PAC\\0' (0x00434150 LE), +0x04 u8 type (0-8), +0x05 u8 last-flag (1 = last entry in chain, 0 = more follow), +0x06 u16 zero, +0x08 u32 unknown (preserved verbatim by repackers via headers.bin), +0x0C u32 length counting from the PAC magic (payload = len - 0x800, payload starts at entry+0x800). UnPAC() loop: next entry at pos += (len + 0x7FF) & ~0x7FF; decompresses ONLY when type == 4 ('if (num == 4) Decode(...)'), all other types saved raw. Sub-files that don't start with 'PAC\\0' are left as-is (SQV MIDI archives or unknown).",
          "PAC TYPE TABLE (brave.txt + jywjyw note.md): 0 = graphics (mostly 32x32 image blocks; 8bpp=64x32, 4bpp=128x32); 1 = graphics/uncompressed script (the memory-resident MAIN/010 entry '1.1' and shop-dialog '2.1' scripts are type 1); 2 = VAB header (.VH); 3 = VAB body (.VB); 4 = LZSS-COMPRESSED DATA (location scripts + embedded MIPS code = the overlays); 5 = unused; 6 = unknown (all data fits in the first 0x800); 7 = unknown; 8 = graphics. SQV format: magic '.sqv', up to 3 u32 offsets to MThd (SMF MIDI) sub-files (value+4, 0 if single), header 16-byte aligned, members 4-byte aligned, VAB file at fixed offset 0x7000 after the MThd data.",
          "EXACT LZSS PARAMETERS (verbatim from brave.c): POS_BITS 10, THRESHOLD 1, N = 1<<10 = 1024-byte ring window, F = (1<<6)+1 = 65 = max match. Flag engine: 'if (!((flags >>= 1) & 0xFF00)) flags = 0xFF00 | encoded[enc_size++];' — flag byte consumed LSB-first, bit 1 = literal byte, bit 0 = 16-bit LE reference. Reference: pos = code & 0x3FF (10 bits), len = (code >> 10) + 1, copies len+1 bytes (loop 'for (i = 0; i <= len; i++)') i.e. true match length = 6-bit field + 2, range 2..65. Copy source: ring[(pos - 1 + i) & (N - 1)] — NOTE the -1 bias: stored positions are ring-index+1 because the game's encoder starts writing at ring index 1 (Vehek: 'the starting position in the buffer appears to be 1'). Decoder write index r starts at 0, r = (r+1) & 1023 after every output byte. Ring is zero-initialized. No decompressed-size field anywhere; CUE grows the output buffer dynamically (start 128 KB, +64 KB).",
          "TERMINATOR — CRITICAL DIFFERENCE: CUE's Decode() has NO terminator; it stops when input length (PAC len - 0x800) is consumed. The GAME's decompressor stops when a reference with 10-bit offset == 0 is read (Vehek, Aug 2014: 'The game stops decompressing when the start-offset of a reference is 0'; streams typically end with bytes 00 00). Vehek also found the PAC header length field has NO effect on the game's decompressed size. Any recompressor must (a) never emit offset-0 references mid-stream (stock Okumura output does, and the game chokes — Vehek's failed 2013 attempt) and (b) terminate with a pos=0 pair. jywjyw's working Compresser.java guarantees this by starting the encoder ring index at r=1.",
          "OVERLAY/MEMORY MAP (jywjyw note.md, Japanese SLPS-01490, same engine as SLUS-00726): EXE at 0x80010000-0x80073fff (file offset = vaddr - 0x8000F800); 0x80078BD0-0x800793D0 = 0x800-byte staging buffer for sector-at-a-time LZSS decompression; 0x800B9480 = SQV/audio; 0x800CDF58-0x80128504 = resident script blob MAIN.CD FILE_010 PAC entry '1.1' (type 1, uncompressed), pointer table in EXE near offset 0x62620; 0x80128508+ = current location's type-4 '0.4' compressed script (~800 KB decompressed), the third (town-dialog) script chains from inside the second; 0x801FF800-0x801FFFFF script runtime scratch. The type-4 blobs CONTAIN MIPS CODE: text pointers are materialized by lui/addiu pairs inside them (Vehek's US example at RAM 0x8017e4dc: '3c04801b lui r4,0x801b / 248417f0 addiu r4,r4,0x17f0'; jywjyw found font-pointer lui/addiu sequences at 0x8013928c/0x8013920c/0x801391ec inside scripts). This exactly explains the AP-world patches at 0x8013xxxx-0x8018xxxx: they target the decompressed type-4 overlay region above 0x80128508, not the EXE. jywjyw's IDA workflow: load EXE, then 'load additional binary' MAIN/010/1.1 at 0x800CDF58 and the location's 0.4 at 0x80128508 — directly replicable in Ghidra.",
          "FULL REPACK REFERENCE EXISTS: https://github.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack (Java/Maven, 2018, by the author of forum thread 26396). Key sources: brm/CdHeader.java (rebuilds .CD TOC: putLong(count), entries start at sector 1, addr += paddedLen/0x800), brm/PacHeader.java (full 0x800 header struct incl. unknown bytes), brm/hack/Compresser.java (binary-search-tree LZSS encoder: POS_BITS=10, THRESHOLD=2, MAX_STORE_LENGTH=65; emits byte0 = pos&0xFF, byte1 = (len-2)<<2 | pos>>8), brm/hack/CdRebuilder.java (rebuilds all 8 .CDs: recompresses '*.4' files, preserves original PAC headers from headers.bin, recomputes len fields, pads to 0x800, warns if a rebuilt .CD exceeds original size), brm/hack/ListCdWriter.java (rebuilds LIST.CD as trimmed concatenation in a 0x1000 buffer), brm/dump/Uncompresser.java + brm/dump/CdSplitter.java (decoder/splitter; names entries {pacIndex}.{type} e.g. '0.4', '1.1', '2.1'). doc/note.md additionally documents the dialog control codes (00 end; 01xx color; 02 3-byte; 03 jump; 04 voice-sync pause; 07 wait-for-input blink; 08 clear; 09xx box style 02=normal 06=yelling; 0A newline; 0E dynamic rescue-count; 17 choice cursor; 20-DF main font, Fxxx kanji, FFxx symbol glyphs, Exxx secondary per-script font) and max 17 chars per dialog line.",
          "FONT (US version, Vehek): main font graphics are in MAIN.CD FILE_010.dir/001 (CUE-extractor naming) starting ~0x4CBC, 1BPP format (JP equivalent at blob offset 0x4c4c -> RAM 0x800d2ba4). US disc layout datapoint (Auryn, 2352-byte-sector image): SC01.CD starts at LBA 6161, ST01.STR at LBA 15965.",
          "FORUM THREADS READ IN FULL: topic 26396 ('[PSX] Brave Fencer Musashi', single post by jywjyw, May 2018) announces the bravefencer-hack repo, notes >100 scripts each containing dialog + a private secondary font + hardcoded memory addresses, references Vehek's finding that text pointers are machine code. Topic 15730 ('[PSX] Brave Fencer Musashi - Messy texts', Jan 2013-Aug 2014, 38 posts over 2 pages) is the thread that produced CUE's tool (announced by Josekenshin in reply #33, Mar 2013) and contains Vehek's LZSS reverse-engineering (replies #29, #36, #37) plus Dark Fulgore's color-code table (reply #28: 0100 white/none, 0101 black, 0102 light red, 0103 pink, 0104 green, 0105 red, 0106 blue, 0107 brown/gold, 0108 gray, 0109 intense red, 010A lime, 010B yellow, 010C intense blue, 010D intense pink, 010E light blue) and Auryn's codes (88xx = voice clip, 0A 19 [14 xx] = newline+pause, speaker-icon bytes xx18: 00=all, 01=Musashi, 02=Steward, 03=princess, 04=butler).",
          "Working Wayback URLs (live romhacking.net is Cloudflare-blocked; utilities page itself has no 2023 snapshot — use the download capture directly): tool RAR http://web.archive.org/web/20131011153707if_/http://www.romhacking.net/download/utilities/939/ ; thread 26396 http://web.archive.org/web/20210412213520/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=26396.0 ; thread 15730 p1 http://web.archive.org/web/20210417055919/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=15730.0 ; p2 http://web.archive.org/web/20210418024205/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=15730.20 (no page 3 — .40 is 404). WebFetch refuses web.archive.org; plain curl works fine, including the CDX API (web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx?url=...) which is flaky (intermittent 503s, retry)."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Download the tool archive: curl -sL -o brave939.rar 'http://web.archive.org/web/20131011153707if_/http://www.romhacking.net/download/utilities/939/' (verify size 16,366 bytes; RAR4 magic 'Rar!').",
          "Extract with 7-Zip (already installed at C:\\Program Files\\7-Zip\\7z.exe): '7z x brave939.rar' -> brave.c, common.inc, brave.exe, brave.txt, readme.txt, license.txt. brave.exe runs natively on Windows, or rebuild with gcc brave.c (common.inc is #included).",
          "Extract MAIN.CD, SC01-SC07.CD, LIST.CD from the SLUS-00726 disc image (2352->2048 byte sector conversion via your ISO tool; US LBAs: SC01.CD at 6161), then run 'brave MAIN.CD SC01.CD ... SC07.CD' (NOT LIST.CD) to produce <name>.CD.dir/FILE_###/ trees with PAC entries split and type-4 entries LZSS-decompressed.",
          "For the splat/Ghidra overlay phase: write (or port) a TOC walker using the confirmed struct (u32 count, u32 zero, then {u32 sector, u32 bytelen} pairs; data sector-relative to .CD start) and PAC walker (0x800 header per entry, type at +4, last-flag at +5, len at +0xC includes the header); decompress only type-4 entries with the exact LZSS (1024 ring, pos=code&0x3FF with -1 bias, len=(code>>10)+2, flags LSB-first 1=literal, stop on pos==0 OR input exhaustion).",
          "Map overlays in Ghidra: import decompressed MAIN FILE_010 type-1 blob at the US equivalent of 0x800CDF58 and the current location's type-4 blob at the US equivalent of 0x80128508 (find the loader pointer table near EXE offset ~0x62620 in the JP EXE; locate the US analogue by searching for the staging-buffer/lui-addiu load code). Verify against known AP-world patch addresses 0x8013xxxx-0x8018xxxx falling inside the second blob.",
          "For any future repack/injection needs, use or port jywjyw/bravefencer-hack: CdRebuilder + Compresser + ListCdWriter implement the full game-compatible rebuild including the pos!=0 encoder constraint and LIST.CD regeneration."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "curl (bundled with Git Bash / Windows) — only client that can reach web.archive.org; Claude Code's WebFetch refuses that domain",
          "7-Zip (installed at C:\\Program Files\\7-Zip\\7z.exe, v26.01) — required because utility #939 is a RAR4, and bsdtar's streaming mode silently fails on it",
          "BRAVE v1.0 (brave.exe Win32, or compile brave.c + common.inc with any gcc/MSVC; GPLv3) — reference extractor/decompressor",
          "jywjyw/bravefencer-hack (Java 8+, Maven, github.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack) — reference recompressor + .CD/LIST.CD rebuilder; doc/note.md is the best memory-map/format document found",
          "Ghidra with PSX loader plugin (already planned) — use 'add to program' style binary imports at 0x800CDF58/0x80128508-equivalent addresses for overlay analysis"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Everything in this gap runs natively on Windows: brave.exe is a Win32 console binary; brave.c compiles with MinGW gcc; 7-Zip handles the RAR; jywjyw's toolchain is Java/Maven (cross-platform). No WSL2 needed for the extraction/format work. (Note for retrieval scripting: Git Bash curl works against web.archive.org; PowerShell Invoke-WebRequest would too.)",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "CUE's extractor names PAC chain entries by TYPE byte ('%03d' % type), so two same-type entries in one chain silently overwrite each other on extraction. jywjyw's CdSplitter names them {index}.{type} ('0.4', '1.1') and is the safer model for the project's own extractor.",
          "brave.txt calls the TOC field 'LBA' but the code proves it is a sector index relative to the start of the .CD file (buffer + entry*0x800), not an absolute disc LBA — do not add the .CD's own disc LBA when splitting.",
          "The PAC len field at +0x0C INCLUDES the 0x800 header (payload = len - 0x800), and the game ignores it for decompression sizing — the real stream terminator is a reference pair with 10-bit pos == 0. CUE's tool instead trusts the length and never checks pos==0; both behaviors must be reconciled when writing the project's extractor (recommended: stop on pos==0, sanity-check against len).",
          "Match-position bias: decoder reads ring[(pos-1+i) & 1023] while writer index starts at 0 — equivalently the encoder's ring origin is 1. Off-by-one here corrupts every decompressed overlay; validate by diffing one type-4 blob against a PCSX-Redux/DuckStation RAM dump at 0x80128508-equivalent.",
          "Recompression is NOT byte-stable: jywjyw's CdRebuilder comment 'recompressed 0.4 script not equals original' — a greedy/BST encoder won't reproduce Square's original bitstream. For matching-decomp purposes treat compressed type-4 payloads as opaque binary in the splat layout; only the DEcompressed overlays need to match.",
          "CUE's Decode() ring[] is a global that is never cleared between files (only r resets) — harmless for the original data but means his tool is not a strict oracle for streams that reference unwritten ring positions (game expects zero-filled ring at offset interpretation start).",
          "All concrete RAM addresses in note.md (0x800CDF58, 0x80128508, EXE offset 0x62620, font offsets 0x4c4c/0x5ccc/0x80d0) are for the JAPANESE SLPS-01490; the USA SLUS-00726 addresses will differ slightly (Vehek's US font offset is 0x4CBC vs JP 0x4c4c) — re-derive in Ghidra, don't copy.",
          "jywjyw's Compresser THRESHOLD=2 vs CUE's THRESHOLD=1 is a naming difference only (CUE adds +1 in the copy loop); both agree actual match length = 6-bit field + 2, range 2..65.",
          "Wayback CDX API intermittently 503s; the 2018+ captures of romhacking.net download URLs are 'Human verification' HTML interstitials — only the 2013-10-11 and 2017-07-31 captures contain the real RAR.",
          "Licensing: brave.c is GPLv3 — fine to study/port for an open decomp toolchain, but note the license if vendoring; Compresser.java carries an LGPL 2.1 header (adapted from JSword's LZSS)."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "PAC types 6 and 7 payload semantics are unknown to every source found (CUE: '???'; jywjyw: '??', noting type 6 keeps all data within its first 0x800 bytes) — will need Ghidra analysis of the loader.",
          "The u32 at PAC header +0x08 is unidentified ('???' in brave.txt; blindly preserved by jywjyw's repacker) — possibly a decompressed-size hint, checksum, or load parameter; worth checking against the game's loader code.",
          "Exact US SLUS-00726 equivalents of the JP overlay load addresses (0x800CDF58 resident blob, 0x80128508 location script) and of the EXE-side pointer table (~JP EXE offset 0x62620) still need to be located in Ghidra.",
          "Which engine routine performs the LZSS decode in the US EXE (Vehek traced it in a debugger but posted no address) — find via the 0x800-byte staging buffer (JP 0x80078BD0) access pattern.",
          "Whether the resident MAIN/010 type-1 blob and the per-location type-4 blobs contain relocations or are position-locked (the lui/addiu hardcoding suggests fully position-locked, which simplifies splat segments).",
          "How the game chooses which subcd/PAC to stream for a given location (the LIST.CD-in-RAM lookup logic) — needed for a complete overlay map."
        ],
        "sources": [
          "http://web.archive.org/web/20131011153707if_/http://www.romhacking.net/download/utilities/939/ (BRAVE v1.0 RAR: brave.c, brave.txt, readme.txt, common.inc read in full)",
          "http://web.archive.org/web/20210412213520if_/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=26396.0 (thread '[PSX] Brave Fencer Musashi', read in full)",
          "http://web.archive.org/web/20210417055919if_/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=15730.0 (thread '[PSX] BFM - Messy texts' page 1)",
          "http://web.archive.org/web/20210418024205if_/https://www.romhacking.net/forum/index.php?topic=15730.20 (page 2 incl. Vehek replies #29/#36/#37, read in full)",
          "https://github.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack (repo tree via api.github.com; README via WebFetch)",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack/master/doc/note.md",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack/master/doc/lzss_explain.gbk.txt",
          "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jywjyw/bravefencer-hack/master/src/main/java/brm/CdHeader.java (+ PacHeader.java, Conf.java, hack/Compresser.java, hack/CdRebuilder.java, hack/ListCdWriter.java, dump/Uncompresser.java)",
          "http://archive.org/wayback/available and http://web.archive.org/cdx/search/cdx (capture discovery; live www.romhacking.net and romhack.ing mirror were unreadable/blocked, noted)"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Which Ghidra MCP server to standardize on for a Claude Code-driven PS1 matching decomp (Brave Fencer Musashi SLUS-00726) on Windows, and what psxrecomp actually ran",
        "key_facts": [
          "RESOLVED: psxrecomp ran GhidrAssistMCP, NOT LaurieWired GhidraMCP. Proof: (a) psxrecomp HANDOFF_PHASE2_2026-04-11.md section 8 lists its MCP tools verbatim: mcp__ghidra__get_binary_info, mcp__ghidra__get_code (format: disassembly or decompiler), mcp__ghidra__disassemble_at, mcp__ghidra__analyze_function, mcp__ghidra__xrefs, mcp__ghidra__get_functions (paginated) — these are exactly GhidrAssistMCP's tool names and exist in no LaurieWired release; (b) github.com/mstan (psxrecomp author) has a fork of GhidrAssistMCP pushed 2026-04-12, mid-project; (c) psxrecomp/.mcp.json is {\"mcpServers\":{\"ghidra\":{\"type\":\"sse\",\"url\":\"http://localhost:7777/sse\"},\"ghidra_psx\":{same URL}}} and GhidrAssistMCP natively serves SSE at /sse with a configurable port (default 8080; 7777 was a user setting); LaurieWired's plugin requires a separate Python bridge and different tool names (decompile_function, list_methods, get_xrefs_to...).",
          "The 1379.tech blog post ('I Built a PS1 Static Recompiler...') links LaurieWired/GhidraMCP in its Tools section — that credit is inaccurate/generic and was the source of the contradiction. The repo evidence overrides the blog.",
          "psxrecomp PLAN.md references older tool names mcp__ghidra__get_function_info, mcp__ghidra__xrefs, and list_data — these were GhidrAssistMCP's pre-2.4.0 names; upstream commit aa3ffc7d 'Align MCP tool names for cross-tool consistency' (2026-03-14, v2.4.0) renamed them. So psxrecomp started on a pre-2.4 build and the HANDOFF doc reflects post-rename 2.5.0-era names. mstan's fork has zero own commits (fork point ~2026-03-29 upstream master) — stock GhidrAssistMCP, port changed in plugin UI only.",
          "GhidrAssistMCP canonical repo moved from jtang613/GhidrAssistMCP to symgraph/GhidrAssistMCP (GitHub redirects; commits authored by Jason Tang). 640 stars, actively maintained. Latest release v2.8.0 (2026-05-29) ships TWO assets: ghidra_12.0_PUBLIC_20260529_GhidrAssistMCP.zip and ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260530_GhidrAssistMCP.zip. README states 'Ghidra 11.4+ (tested with Ghidra 12.0 Public)'. 38 tools. Transports: SSE (http://localhost:8080/sse), SSE message (/message), and Streamable HTTP (/mcp) — no Python bridge needed; Claude Code connects directly via .mcp.json.",
          "GhidrAssistMCP has the data-type tooling a matching decomp needs: 'struct' tool actions = create (from C definition or empty), modify, merge, set_field (single field at offset), name_gap, auto_create (build struct from variable usage), rename_field, field_xrefs; 'types' tool actions = list, get_info, set (apply data type at address, arrays via array_count or 'int[16]' suffix), delete; 'variables' tool = list, rename (local or global via scope param), set_type, set_prototype; plus rename_symbol (target_type: function/data/variable), batch_rename, create_data_var, create_function, get_code (decompile/disassemble), xrefs, search_bytes, patch_bytes, assemble_code, export_program. Headless since v2.3.0 (2026-03-07).",
          "GhidrAssistMCP headless launch (works on Windows with analyzeHeadless.bat): analyzeHeadless <projects-dir> McpHeadless -import <binary> -scriptPath \"<ext>/ghidra_scripts\" -preScript GAMCPStartServerScript.java \"host=127.0.0.1\" \"port=8080\" \"wait=true\" (wait=true keeps the session open for interactive MCP clients).",
          "LaurieWired/GhidraMCP is dead for this purpose: 9,185 stars but last commit/release 1.4 on 2025-06-23 ('Fixed for Ghidra 11.3.2'), 80 open issues. Issue #83 (2025-08-22, OPEN): v1.4 extension manifest rejected by Ghidra 11.4.1 (ModuleName/ModuleDescription errors), no fix. Issue #131 (2026-02-22, OPEN): 'Update for ghidra 12.1' — no response, nothing merged. Stock 1.4 caps you at Ghidra 11.3.2, which ghidra_psx_ldr no longer targets.",
          "bethington/ghidra-mcp (standalone repo, not a GitHub fork, but uses LaurieWired-style architecture: Java plugin HTTP server port 8089 + bridge_mcp_ghidra.py Python bridge): 2,384 stars, v5.13.1 (2026-06-08), pushed 2026-06-09. README now claims Ghidra 12.1 primary support (the '12.0.3' claim is outdated), 249 MCP tools including create_struct, create_union, create_enum, add_struct_field, modify_struct_field, apply_data_type, set_global_variable_type, clone_data_type, rename_function_by_address, rename_global_variable, batch_decompile. Transports: stdio (via bridge), streamable-http, sse (deprecated). Headless server: java -jar GhidraMCPHeadless.jar; Docker compose on port 8089. Windows install: python -m tools.setup ensure-prereqs --ghidra-path \"C:\\ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC\" / build / deploy. Claims 'lazy tool loading' to mitigate the 249-tool context cost. Unaudited, extremely fast version churn.",
          "clearbluejar/pyghidra-mcp (PyPI: pyghidra-mcp; pip install pyghidra-mcp or uvx pyghidra-mcp): headless-first, multi-binary Ghidra-project support (import_binary, list_project_binaries), transports stdio (default) / streamable-http (127.0.0.1:8000/mcp) / sse (legacy). Tools: decompile_function, search_code, list_xrefs, gen_callgraph, read_bytes, search_strings/symbols, rename_function, rename_variable, set_variable_type, set_function_prototype, set_comment. NO struct-creation or apply-data-type-at-address tools — insufficient alone for matching-decomp data work. Windows config: set GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR env var.",
          "ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12: 1 star, last push 2026-02-19, targets Ghidra 12.0.1/JDK 21 only — a one-off LaurieWired recompile, no struct tools, not viable.",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr (lab313ru) latest release 2026.06.04 ships per-version zips including ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260604_ghidra_psx_ldr.zip (also 12.0 through 12.0.4). So a Ghidra 12.1 stack is fully achievable: Ghidra 12.1 + ghidra_psx_ldr 2026.06.04 (12.1 zip) + GhidrAssistMCP 2.8.0 (12.1 zip).",
          "Ghidra current releases: 12.1.2 (2026-06-05, ghidra_12.1.2_PUBLIC_20260605.zip) and 12.1 (2026-05-13, ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260513.zip). Both extension zips above are built against '12.1_PUBLIC', so pin Ghidra 12.1 exactly (not 12.1.2) unless you rebuild the extensions from source (gradle installExtension with GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR set). Ghidra 12.x requires JDK 21.",
          "RECOMMENDATION: standardize on GhidrAssistMCP v2.8.0 + Ghidra 12.1 PUBLIC + ghidra_psx_ldr 2026.06.04 (12.1 asset). Rationale: it is the server the psxrecomp proof-of-concept actually validated with Claude Code over SSE; it is actively maintained with Ghidra 12.1 builds; its 38 tools fit Claude's context budget while covering struct creation/merge/auto_create, apply-type-at-address, global+local rename, prototypes, decompile, xrefs; no Python bridge process to babysit on Windows. Keep bethington/ghidra-mcp 5.13.x as documented fallback if a missing niche tool (e.g., create_enum at scale, batch_decompile) becomes a bottleneck, and pyghidra-mcp for scripted headless batch passes.",
          "Claude Code project config (.mcp.json at repo root) for the recommended setup: {\"mcpServers\": {\"ghidra\": {\"type\": \"sse\", \"url\": \"http://localhost:8080/sse\"}}} — server name 'ghidra' yields tool names mcp__ghidra__get_binary_info etc., matching psxrecomp's docs. Do NOT copy psxrecomp's duplicate 'ghidra_psx' entry (it points at the same URL and just duplicates every tool)."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Install JDK 21 (e.g., Temurin: winget install EclipseAdoptium.Temurin.21.JDK).",
          "Download and extract ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260513.zip from https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/releases/tag/Ghidra_12.1_build (pin 12.1, not 12.1.2, to match extension builds).",
          "Download ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260604_ghidra_psx_ldr.zip from https://github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr/releases/tag/2026.06.04 and ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260530_GhidrAssistMCP.zip from https://github.com/symgraph/GhidrAssistMCP/releases/tag/2.8.0.",
          "In Ghidra: File -> Install Extensions -> + -> select both zips -> restart Ghidra. Then File -> Configure -> Configure Plugins -> enable GhidrAssistMCP.",
          "Open the CodeBrowser control panel Window -> GhidrAssistMCP and set Host=localhost, Port=8080 (or 7777 to mirror psxrecomp); the server exposes /sse, /message, /mcp.",
          "Import the BFM main executable (SLUS_007.26 from the USA disc) via ghidra_psx_ldr's PSX-EXE loader (it sets base 0x80010000, MIPS R3000, and offers PsyQ SDK signature/type application — pick the SDK version once identified).",
          "Create .mcp.json in the decomp repo root: {\"mcpServers\": {\"ghidra\": {\"type\": \"sse\", \"url\": \"http://localhost:8080/sse\"}}}; verify in Claude Code with /mcp that ~38 mcp__ghidra__* tools appear (get_binary_info, get_code, xrefs, struct, types, variables, rename_symbol, batch_rename, ...).",
          "For unattended/batch passes, run headless instead of the GUI: <ghidra>\\support\\analyzeHeadless.bat <projects-dir> McpHeadless -process SLUS_007.26 -scriptPath \"<extension-dir>/ghidra_scripts\" -preScript GAMCPStartServerScript.java \"host=127.0.0.1\" \"port=8080\" \"wait=true\".",
          "Document in the project context doc that struct work goes through the 'struct' tool (create from C definition / auto_create / merge), type application through 'types' action=set at address, and global retyping through variables rename/set_type with scope=global — and that PsyQ .gdt archives must be attached manually in the GUI data type manager first (no MCP tool opens archives)."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "JDK 21 (Temurin) — required by Ghidra 12.x; winget install EclipseAdoptium.Temurin.21.JDK",
          "Ghidra 12.1 PUBLIC (ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260513.zip) — github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra releases; plain zip extract, no installer",
          "GhidrAssistMCP v2.8.0, asset ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260530_GhidrAssistMCP.zip — github.com/symgraph/GhidrAssistMCP (formerly jtang613); install via Ghidra File -> Install Extensions; 38 MCP tools, SSE/streamable-HTTP on configurable port (default 8080)",
          "ghidra_psx_ldr release 2026.06.04, asset ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC_20260604_ghidra_psx_ldr.zip — github.com/lab313ru/ghidra_psx_ldr; PSX-EXE loader + PsyQ signatures; same extension-install path",
          "Claude Code with project .mcp.json SSE entry (type: sse, url http://localhost:8080/sse) — no Python bridge process needed",
          "FALLBACK ONLY: bethington/ghidra-mcp v5.13.1 (249 tools incl. create_struct/apply_data_type/set_global_variable_type; python -m tools.setup ensure-prereqs/build/deploy --ghidra-path; needs Python bridge bridge_mcp_ghidra.py, plugin HTTP on 8089) and clearbluejar/pyghidra-mcp (pip/uvx install; headless multi-binary; no struct tools)",
          "DO NOT USE: LaurieWired/GhidraMCP 1.4 (Ghidra 11.3.2 max, unmaintained since 2025-06-23, issues #83/#131 open) and ismaelcaraballo-afk/GhidraMCP-12 (1 star, stale, 12.0.1 only)"
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Everything runs natively on Windows 11 — WSL2 is NOT needed for the Ghidra/MCP stack. Ghidra 12.1 + JDK 21 are native; GhidrAssistMCP is a pure Java Ghidra extension whose embedded SSE/HTTP server Claude Code reaches over localhost (psxrecomp itself ran this on Windows — its docs reference 'C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Nmap\\ncat' Git-Bash paths); headless mode uses analyzeHeadless.bat. bethington/ghidra-mcp documents a Windows-first install (python -m tools.setup ... --ghidra-path \"F:\\ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC\") plus optional Docker. pyghidra-mcp runs on Windows via pip with GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR set ([System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('GHIDRA_INSTALL_DIR','C:\\path\\to\\ghidra')). The only thing likely to want WSL2 later is the matching-build toolchain (PsyQ cc1/aspsx under wine/wibo or mips-gcc), which is outside this MCP question.",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "The 1379.tech blog credits LaurieWired/GhidraMCP, but the repo evidence proves GhidrAssistMCP was the real server — any context doc citing the blog's tool list verbatim will reproduce the error this research just corrected.",
          "Extension version locking: both 12.1 zips are built against ghidra_12.1_PUBLIC; installing them on Ghidra 12.1.2 may trip Ghidra's extension-version check (red 'incompatible' flag, like LaurieWired issue #83 on 11.4.1). Pin Ghidra 12.1 exactly, or rebuild extensions from source (gradle installExtension) when moving to 12.1.x point releases.",
          "GhidrAssistMCP has no MCP tool to open/import a .gdt data-type archive — attaching the PsyQ .gdt (or running ghidra_psx_ldr's PsyQ type application) is a one-time manual GUI step; after that, the 'types' and 'struct' tools can reference those types by name (verify this resolution works early).",
          "psxrecomp's .mcp.json defines two server names (ghidra, ghidra_psx) pointing at the same URL — that duplicates all 38 tools in Claude's context. Use a single 'ghidra' entry.",
          "GhidrAssistMCP renamed its tools in v2.4.0 (2026-03-14): get_function_info/list_data (seen in psxrecomp PLAN.md and in older write-ups) no longer exist; current names are analyze_function/get_data_vars/get_code etc. Write the context doc against the v2.8.0 tool names.",
          "bethington/ghidra-mcp's 249 tools risk swamping Claude Code's tool context despite claimed lazy loading, the codebase is single-maintainer with day-apart releases (5.13.0 -> 5.13.1), and it adds a Python bridge process — treat as unvetted fallback, not foundation.",
          "pyghidra-mcp cannot create structs or apply types at addresses (only set_variable_type/set_function_prototype) — fine for batch decompile/search, dead end as the primary server for matching-decomp data work.",
          "psxrecomp validated this stack against a BIOS/recompiler workflow, not a matching decomp — it never exercised heavy struct creation; GhidrAssistMCP's struct/types tools are present but their ergonomics under matching-decomp load are unproven.",
          "Headless GhidrAssistMCP requires 'wait=true' or the analyzeHeadless process exits after the prescript; also -process (not -import) on subsequent runs to reuse the project."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "Which PsyQ SDK version Brave Fencer Musashi (1998) was built with (likely 4.x — needed to pick the right ghidra_psx_ldr signature set and PsyQ .gdt); determine via SLUS_007.26 library-signature scan or string evidence.",
          "Whether GhidrAssistMCP 2.8.0's 12.1 zip installs cleanly on Ghidra 12.1.2 (untested here) — affects whether to track Ghidra point releases or pin 12.1.",
          "Whether GhidrAssistMCP's 'types' action=set can reference types living in an attached .gdt archive or only types already copied into the program's data type manager (needs a 5-minute hands-on test).",
          "Exact GhidrAssistMCP version psxrecomp ran (evidence brackets it between pre-2.4.0 and 2.5.0; mstan's fork point is upstream master ~2026-03-29) — historical detail only, does not affect the recommendation.",
          "Real-world quality of bethington/ghidra-mcp's 249 tools (claims unaudited; no third-party review found) if it's ever promoted from fallback."
        ],
        "sources": [
          "https://github.com/mstan/psxrecomp",
