      },
      {
        "topic": "Windows 11 + WSL2 split-brain workflow facts for BFM-decomp: filesystem placement (9P penalty, inotify), cross-boundary git, wsl.exe invocation from Windows Claude Code, and WSL<->Windows networking for GhidraMCP on 127.0.0.1:8080",
        "key_facts": [
          "LOCAL MACHINE STATE: WSL is NOT installed yet on this PC. Running wsl.exe --list prints 'The Windows Subsystem for Linux is not installed. You can install by running wsl.exe --install'. Install with: wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04 (elevated PowerShell; may require reboot). Host is Windows 11 Pro 25H2 build 26200 — comfortably above the 22H2 requirement for mirrored networking, dnsTunneling, autoProxy, and Hyper-V firewall features.",
          "LOCAL MACHINE STATE: Z: is a local fixed NTFS drive (Win32_LogicalDisk DriveType=3, FileSystem=NTFS, volume '5TB'), so once WSL is installed it will be automounted via DrvFs at /mnt/z by default ([automount] enabled=true, root=/mnt/ — learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/wsl-config). The existing repo Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp will therefore be visible in WSL at /mnt/z/Storage/git/BFM-decomp, but only over 9P (slow, no inotify).",
          "FILESYSTEM PERFORMANCE (official wording): learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/filesystems: 'We recommend against working across operating systems with your files... For the fastest performance speed, store your files in the WSL file system if you are working in a Linux command line... Use the Linux file system root directory: /home/<user name>/Project. Not the Windows file system root directory: /mnt/c/Users/<user name>/Project'. learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/compare-versions lists 'Performance across OS file systems' as the one ❌ for WSL2 and says it 'can be addressed by storing your project files on the same operating system as the tools you are running'.",
          "FILESYSTEM PERFORMANCE (numbers): Microsoft does not publish an exact /mnt 9P penalty multiplier. Documented quantification: WSL2 ext4 is 'up to 20x faster compared to WSL 1 when unpacking a zipped tarball, and around 2-5x faster when using git clone, npm install and cmake' (compare-versions), and compare-versions explicitly says cross-OS file access is FASTER under WSL1 than WSL2 — i.e. WSL2 /mnt/* is the slowest path. Community benchmark corroboration: forevolve.com measured builds up to 375% faster after moving a project from /mnt/c to the WSL ext4 filesystem. Treat /mnt/z builds as roughly 5-20x slower for file-intensive work (make, splat extract, git status).",
          "INOTIFY / FILE WATCHERS: cross-OS change notifications do not propagate in either direction. (a) Linux inotify watchers on /mnt/<drive> (9P/DrvFs) do not receive events for changes made by Windows apps — microsoft/WSL issue #4739 ('File changes made by Windows apps on Windows filesystem don't trigger notifications for Linux apps') and #5424. (b) Windows watchers (ReadDirectoryChangesW/FindFirstChangeNotification, i.e. anything VS Code/Ghidra would use) get NO events on \\\\wsl$\\ / \\\\wsl.localhost\\ paths because the Plan 9 protocol servers lack change-notification support — microsoft/WSL issue #4581. Consequence: asm-differ watch mode (which uses file watching on build artifacts) only works if BOTH the source tree and the build outputs live on ext4 and are modified from inside Linux.",
          "GIT ACROSS THE BOUNDARY: learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/tutorials/wsl-git: 'If you are working with the same repository folder between Windows, WSL, or a container, be sure to set up consistent line endings... Git may report a large number of modified files that have no differences aside from their line endings... you can disable line ending conversion using a .gitattributes file or globally on the Windows side.' Windows git defaults core.autocrlf=true; WSL git defaults to no conversion — sharing one working tree makes every file appear modified to one side. Best practice for a decomp repo (byte-exact asm/source): commit a .gitattributes with '* text=auto eol=lf' (or '* -text') rather than relying on per-side autocrlf; on Windows set core.autocrlf=false or input.",
          "GIT FILE MODES: DrvFs mounts have metadata DISABLED by default (wsl-config [automount] options, metadata default 'disabled'), so chmod bits are not persisted on /mnt/z and files surface with synthesized modes — WSL git on an NTFS working tree will see spurious 100644<->100755 mode changes unless core.filemode=false is set in that clone. Also note the two sides keep separate global configs (%UserProfile%\\.gitconfig vs \\\\wsl$\\<distro>\\home\\$USER\\.gitconfig — quoted in wsl-git tutorial), and each side's git invalidates the other's stat cache/index, making git status slow and noisy on a shared tree. Credential sharing: install latest Git for Windows and point WSL git at GCM ('It is recommended to install the latest Git for Windows in order to share credentials & settings between WSL and the Windows host').",
          "RECOMMENDED LAYOUT (rationale from the above): two clones, one per OS. (1) Decomp build repo: clone INSIDE WSL at ~/bfm-decomp (ext4) — this is where splat extract, make, gcc/maspsx, asm-differ watch all run; pushing/pulling through the shared GitHub remote (or a local bare repo) syncs with the Windows side. (2) Ghidra project (.gpr/.rep), GhidraMCP plugin, and Claude Code stay on Windows NTFS at Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp. Do NOT host the Ghidra project on \\\\wsl.localhost\\... : Ghidra's own docs recommend local-drive storage (server README: 'It is highly recommended that the installation files... reside on a local drive'), Ghidra uses ~lock files that are unreliable over network redirectors, and \\\\wsl.localhost is a 9P network share with no change notifications and slow IO. One-off file transfer across the boundary (e.g. copying the SLUS-00726 executable or splat-extracted overlays into the Ghidra import folder) is fine via /mnt/z/... from Linux or \\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu-24.04\\home\\<user>\\bfm-decomp\\... from Windows — it is sustained/random IO and watchers that are broken, not correctness (with one Claude Code-specific caveat, see risks).",
          "WSL INVOCATION FROM WINDOWS: canonical daily-loop command from PowerShell/Claude Code: wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd /home/<user>/bfm-decomp -- bash -lc 'make -j$(nproc)' . Flag semantics (wsl --help, mirrored in microsoft/WSL issue threads): '--cd <Directory>: Sets the specified directory as the current working directory. If ~ is used the Linux user's home path will be used. If the path begins with a / character, it will be interpreted as an absolute Linux path. Otherwise, the value must be an absolute Windows path.' Everything after the bare '--' is passed to the default shell unmodified; per learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/filesystems 'The commands passed into wsl.exe are forwarded to the WSL process without modification. File paths must be specified in the WSL format.' Use -e/--exec to run a binary without a shell. PowerShell quoting gotcha: wrap the Linux command in SINGLE quotes — in double quotes PowerShell itself expands $(nproc)/$vars before wsl.exe sees them.",
          "EXIT CODES: wsl.exe propagates the Linux command's exit status back to the Windows caller ($LASTEXITCODE in PowerShell, %ERRORLEVEL% in cmd) for standard distros like Ubuntu — verified behavior discussed in nix-community/NixOS-WSL issue #138 (where Ubuntu works and NixOS was the broken exception). So 'wsl.exe ... -- make ...; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) {...}' is a reliable pattern. Caveat: wsl.exe also returns nonzero (typically 1 or 0xFFFFFFFF) for its own launcher errors (bad distro name, WSL not installed), indistinguishable from a build failure except via stderr text. wsl.exe output on Windows is UTF-16LE — observed locally as interleaved-null garbling when piped; set $env:WSL_UTF8=1 or pipe through Out-String when capturing.",
          "CWD MAPPING / UNC LIMITS: binaries invoked via 'wsl <cmd>' 'use the same working directory as the current CMD or PowerShell prompt' (filesystems doc) — a Windows cwd is mapped to /mnt/<drive>/...; prefer explicit --cd to avoid accidentally building under /mnt. cmd.exe cannot use UNC paths (\\\\wsl.localhost\\...) as a current directory (classic 'CMD does not support UNC paths as current directories' limitation); PowerShell can cd into \\\\wsl.localhost. If wsl.exe is launched from an untranslatable cwd it warns and falls back to the Linux home directory.",
          "NETWORKING — NAT (default): direction matters. Windows -> WSL: 'you can access it from a Windows app... using localhost' (localhost forwarding; .wslconfig [wsl2] localhostForwarding default=true, 'ignored when networkingMode=mirrored'). WSL -> Windows: localhost does NOT reach Windows services; learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/networking: 'If you want to access a networking app running on Windows... from your Linux distribution... you need to use the IP address of your host machine', obtained INSIDE WSL with: ip route show | grep -i default | awk '{ print $3}' (e.g. 172.30.96.1). Do NOT use /etc/resolv.conf for this on Windows 11 22H2+: dnsTunneling is on by default and pins the nameserver to 10.255.255.254, which is not the host vEthernet IP — the docs' resolv.conf trick is stale; 'ip route' is the reliable method.",
          "NETWORKING — server binding + firewall: the networking doc warns that connections from the WSL VM 'will be treated as connections from the Local Area Network (LAN)... you may need to bind your application to 0.0.0.0 instead of 127.0.0.1'. GhidraMCP needs NO change: its plugin creates the server with 'server = HttpServer.create(new InetSocketAddress(port), 0)' (wildcard bind, all interfaces; default port 8080, configurable at Edit -> Tool Options -> 'GhidraMCP HTTP Server' -> 'Server Port' — LaurieWired/GhidraMCP source + README). The remaining blocker is Windows Defender/Hyper-V firewall on the vEthernet (WSL) interface; allow inbound with e.g. New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'GhidraMCP from WSL' -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 8080 -InterfaceAlias 'vEthernet (WSL (Hyper-V firewall))' -Action Allow, or on Win11 22H2+ the Hyper-V firewall cmdlets: Set-NetFirewallHyperVVMSetting -Name '{40E0AC32-46A5-438A-A0B2-2B479E8F2E90}' -DefaultInboundAction Allow.",
          "NETWORKING — mirrored mode (recommended for this project): in %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig set [wsl2] networkingMode=mirrored (requires Windows 11 22H2+; this machine is 25H2). Documented benefit: 'Connect to Windows servers from within Linux using the localhost address 127.0.0.1' (IPv6 ::1 not supported) — i.e. a WSL-side decompile.py/curl can hit the GhidraMCP server at http://127.0.0.1:8080/ directly, same URL as Windows-side Claude Code uses. Optional [experimental] hostAddressLoopback=true additionally allows reaching the host via its assigned LAN IPs. Apply config changes with wsl --shutdown and wait ~8 seconds (the documented '8 second rule') before relaunching.",
          "GhidraMCP topology note: the MCP bridge script bridge_mcp_ghidra.py defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8080/ and accepts --ghidra-server <url> (plus --transport/--mcp-host/--mcp-port). Since Claude Code and Ghidra both run on Windows, the MCP path never crosses the WSL boundary — the networking facts above only matter for any WSL-side script (build hooks, asm-differ helpers) that wants to query Ghidra's HTTP API directly.",
          "Windows access path to the WSL tree: \\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu-24.04\\home\\<user>\\bfm-decomp (modern name; \\\\wsl$\\ is the legacy alias, both documented). 'explorer.exe .' inside WSL opens the current Linux directory in Explorer. Windows-side Read/diff of files there is fine; sustained git/build/watch operations over it are not."
        ],
        "workflow_steps": [
          "Install WSL2 + distro (one-time, elevated PowerShell): wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04 ; reboot if prompted; verify with wsl -l -v (distro name must match exactly — it is 'Ubuntu-24.04', check before hardcoding in scripts).",
          "Create %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig with: [wsl2] newline networkingMode=mirrored (machine is 25H2 so supported), then wsl --shutdown and wait ~8s. This makes http://127.0.0.1:8080/ (GhidraMCP) reachable from inside WSL with zero per-boot IP discovery. Fallback if mirrored causes problems: stay on NAT and have WSL scripts resolve the host with: ip route show | grep -i default | awk '{ print $3}' — and add a Windows firewall inbound-allow for TCP 8080 on the vEthernet (WSL) interface.",
          "Clone the build repo INSIDE WSL ext4, not on /mnt/z: wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd ~ -- bash -lc 'git clone <remote> bfm-decomp' . Keep Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp as the Windows-side checkout for Ghidra project files, notes, and Claude Code's cwd; sync the two through the git remote, never by editing one working tree from both OSes.",
          "Commit a .gitattributes (e.g. '* text=auto eol=lf', binaries marked -text) so line endings are repo-enforced; in the WSL clone set git config core.filemode true (ext4 is fine); if any clone must ever live on /mnt/z set core.filemode=false there.",
          "Daily build loop from Windows-side Claude Code: wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd /home/<user>/bfm-decomp -- bash -lc 'make extract && make -j$(nproc)' — single-quote the bash command so PowerShell does not expand $(...); check $LASTEXITCODE for pass/fail.",
          "Run asm-differ watch mode (python3 diff.py -mwo <func>) in an interactive WSL terminal (Windows Terminal Ubuntu tab), entirely on ext4 — its file-watching will not fire for files on /mnt/* or files edited from Windows over \\\\wsl.localhost (WSL #4739/#4581). Editing source from Windows is OK only if the watcher is pointed at build outputs regenerated by Linux-side make; safest is to edit within the WSL tree too (VS Code Remote-WSL or Claude Code driving wsl.exe).",
          "Keep the Ghidra project (.gpr/.rep) and GhidraMCP plugin on Windows NTFS; import the target binary by copying it once from the WSL tree (\\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu-24.04\\home\\<user>\\bfm-decomp\\... or wsl.exe -- cp ... /mnt/z/...). All Ghidra MCP calls from Claude Code stay Windows-local at http://127.0.0.1:8080/ (Tool Options -> GhidraMCP HTTP Server -> Server Port).",
          "After any .wslconfig or /etc/wsl.conf change: close shells, wsl --shutdown, wait 8 seconds (documented rule), relaunch."
        ],
        "tools_required": [
          "WSL2 (NOT currently installed on this machine) — install via 'wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04' from elevated PowerShell; keep updated with 'wsl --update' (Microsoft Store servicing).",
          "Ubuntu-24.04 distro (WSL2) hosting the build toolchain: make, splat, mips gcc/cc1 (psyq/gcc 2.x toolchain per decomp setup), maspsx, asm-differ, python3.",
          "Windows-native Ghidra + GhidraMCP plugin (LaurieWired/GhidraMCP; HTTP server default port 8080, wildcard-bound, configurable in Edit -> Tool Options -> 'GhidraMCP HTTP Server'); bridge_mcp_ghidra.py with --ghidra-server http://127.0.0.1:8080/ for Claude Code MCP.",
          "Git on BOTH sides: Git for Windows (latest, includes Git Credential Manager) and apt git inside Ubuntu; WSL git configured to use GCM for shared credentials if pushing from WSL.",
          "PowerShell (Claude Code host shell) using wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd ... -- bash -lc '...' as the cross-boundary command runner.",
          "Optional: %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig with networkingMode=mirrored (Windows 11 22H2+; this machine is 25H2) for two-way localhost."
        ],
        "windows_vs_linux": "Runs natively on Windows: Ghidra GUI + GhidraMCP plugin (HTTP :8080), the MCP bridge (bridge_mcp_ghidra.py), Claude Code itself, git for the Windows checkout at Z:\\Storage\\git\\BFM-decomp, and the Ghidra project files (.gpr/.rep — keep on NTFS; do not host on \\\\wsl.localhost, which is a 9P network redirector with no change notifications and lock-file risk). Requires Linux/WSL2: the entire matching-decomp build pipeline — splat extraction, make, the PSX gcc toolchain, maspsx, asm-differ watch mode, any inotify-based tooling — and its repo clone must live on WSL ext4 (~/bfm-decomp), per Microsoft's explicit guidance against cross-OS file access (filesystems doc) and because inotify does not work for cross-OS changes on /mnt/* (WSL #4739) and Windows watchers get no events over \\\\wsl.localhost (WSL #4581). Boundary crossings: commands via wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd <linux-path> -- bash -lc '...' (exit codes propagate to $LASTEXITCODE); files via /mnt/z (Z: is a local fixed NTFS drive, auto-mounted) or \\\\wsl.localhost\\Ubuntu-24.04\\... for one-off copies only; network via mirrored mode (WSL can then reach Windows GhidraMCP at 127.0.0.1:8080) or, under default NAT, via the host IP from `ip route show | grep -i default | awk '{ print $3}'` plus a firewall allow on the vEthernet (WSL) interface — WSL->Windows localhost does NOT work under NAT, while Windows->WSL localhost does (localhostForwarding default true).",
        "risks_gotchas": [
          "Building on /mnt/z instead of ~/ in WSL silently works but is the documented worst-performing path (WSL2 cross-OS slower than even WSL1; ext4 is 2-20x faster per Microsoft's own numbers) — every make/git/splat operation pays the 9P round-trip tax.",
          "asm-differ/inotify watch mode silently never fires for changes made from the Windows side or on /mnt/* (microsoft/WSL #4739, #5424); Windows file watchers likewise get zero events on \\\\wsl.localhost (microsoft/WSL #4581). Watch tooling failing 'quietly' looks like a hung diff, not an error.",
          "Claude Code-specific 9P bug: anthropics/claude-code issue #28015 reports Write/Edit on WSL2 drvfs mounts can hit a statx/9P cache-poisoning kernel bug creating 'ghost files' (ENOENT for files that exist). Another reason Claude Code (Windows-side) should not edit the WSL tree through /mnt-style paths from a WSL-side agent, and why per-OS clones are safer.",
          "Sharing ONE working tree across OSes invites: CRLF mass-modification noise (Windows core.autocrlf=true vs WSL), spurious file-mode diffs (DrvFs metadata disabled by default, so chmod bits aren't persisted; fix core.filemode=false), and constant git index stat-cache invalidation making git status slow on both sides. Use two clones + remote sync.",
          "Under default NAT, a WSL script pointed at http://127.0.0.1:8080/ for GhidraMCP fails to connect with no helpful error — localhost only forwards Windows->WSL, not WSL->Windows. Also do not scrape /etc/resolv.conf for the host IP on Win11 22H2+: dnsTunneling (default on) pins it to 10.255.255.254; use `ip route show | grep -i default`.",
          "Even with the host IP correct, Windows Defender/Hyper-V firewall typically blocks inbound from the WSL subnet; GhidraMCP binds wildcard (InetSocketAddress(port)) so the fix is purely a firewall allow rule — and that wildcard bind also means port 8080 is exposed to the LAN; consider scoping the rule or relying on mirrored mode + loopback instead.",
          "PowerShell quoting: wsl.exe ... -- bash -lc \"make -j$(nproc)\" breaks because PowerShell evaluates $(nproc) itself; use single quotes. wsl.exe emits UTF-16LE output (observed garbled when captured); set WSL_UTF8=1 or normalize when parsing build logs.",
          "wsl.exe launcher errors (wrong distro name, WSL not running) also return nonzero exit codes — distinguish from build failures by stderr. Distro name is 'Ubuntu-24.04' (capital U, hyphenated) for store installs; verify with wsl -l -v before hardcoding.",
          ".wslconfig/wsl.conf edits do not take effect until the VM fully stops: wsl --shutdown + ~8 second wait ('8 second rule'); forgetting this makes networkingMode/mirrored experiments look broken.",
          "cmd.exe cannot cd into \\\\wsl.localhost (UNC cwd unsupported); use PowerShell for any Windows-side poking at the WSL tree. Ghidra project files must not live there: 9P share + Ghidra ~lock files + no change notifications = corruption/locking risk; Ghidra docs recommend local-drive storage.",
          "Case sensitivity mismatch: ext4 is case-sensitive, NTFS/DrvFs case-insensitive by default — two files differing only by case in the WSL repo would collide if ever copied to the Windows side (relevant for extracted asset filenames)."
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "Does any WSL-side tool actually need to call GhidraMCP over HTTP (e.g. a decompile.py fetching pseudo-C during builds)? If all Ghidra access flows through Windows-side Claude Code MCP, mirrored networking is a nice-to-have rather than a requirement.",
          "Mirrored mode vs NAT stability: mirrored is recommended here and supported on 25H2, but some VPN/virtualization stacks still misbehave with it; needs a 5-minute smoke test (curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/ from WSL with Ghidra running) after install, with the NAT + ip-route + firewall-rule recipe as fallback.",
          "Exact post-install distro name and default user must be confirmed with wsl -l -v / whoami before the context doc hardcodes wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-24.04 --cd /home/<user>/... commands.",
          "Where to put the canonical git remote for the two-clone sync (GitHub private repo vs a bare repo on Z:) — a bare repo on Z: accessed from WSL via /mnt/z works for push/pull (one-shot IO is fine over 9P) but a hosted remote is simpler and safer.",
          "Whether the project will ever want Windows-side asm-differ/objdiff GUI tooling against WSL build artifacts; if so, plan a copy-out step (build artifacts -> Z:) since watchers won't cross the boundary."
        ],
